727
01:08:54,446 --> 01:08:57,956
So let's start with an overview of what a data center is.

728
01:08:59,054 --> 01:09:08,928
In traditional IT, in the pre-cloud era and still ongoing, servers and applications are hosted in client-specific,

729
01:09:09,168 --> 01:09:12,676
purpose-designed or purpose-built IT data centers.

730
01:09:13,774 --> 01:09:21,124
And if we get into a data center, we will see aisles like these ones, and you will see cabinets as you can see.

731
01:09:22,030 --> 01:09:26,980
And if you open one of the cabinets and look from the front and the back, you will see cables

732
01:09:28,110 --> 01:09:33,988
on the back side, and you will see the details of the servers that are mounted on the front side.

733
01:09:34,382 --> 01:09:42,368
In general, any data center is a considerable space size, real state size, a building basically, or a floor in a building, or

734
01:09:42,528 --> 01:09:52,324
could be the entire building that is air conditioned, physically secured, and manned 24-7 to ensure continuous operation.

735
01:09:52,462 --> 01:09:58,116
And this is where the servers will be placed. Of course, there has to be power and redundant power and so on.

736
01:09:58,288 --> 01:10:04,036
And servers are going to be connected to a network, and this is how they function basically.

737
01:10:04,462 --> 01:10:05,860
We're going to touch on

738
01:10:06,574 --> 01:10:15,012
data centers again when we get to the networking component because we need to talk about what is a data center network or DCN is, but that will come later on.

739
01:10:15,470 --> 01:10:18,276
Let's continue with our overview of what a data center is.

740
01:10:20,046 --> 01:10:26,916
The image that you see in front of you right now, this is the AWS data center in Mumbai, in India.

741
01:10:27,566 --> 01:10:37,092
So this is how big that is. So if you look at the size of the trees, for example, compared to the size of the buildings that are available in data center

742
01:10:37,456 --> 01:10:43,536
and the redundant power and the generators and all the different utilities that get into the data center,

743
01:10:44,336 --> 01:10:46,212
you'll understand what the size is.

744
01:10:46,542 --> 01:10:53,540
So, for example, look at this car, this vehicle, compared to the size of the building, and then you will appreciate how big that would be.

745
01:10:56,910 --> 01:11:07,236
So we talked before about physical servers, and we showed the images of some rack mountable servers, the one that are flat basically. They are not high, they are flat.

746
01:11:08,174 --> 01:11:10,816
So the servers that are flat they have

747
01:11:13,230 --> 01:11:19,140
angles or brackets on the side, and that's how they get mounted. So this is called a rack or a cabinet,

748
01:11:19,886 --> 01:11:24,516
and each one of these rows that you see here is a rack mounted server.

749
01:11:24,814 --> 01:11:33,252
And the rails on the side, the silver components here, the rails on the side is where the server gets fixed or bolted to the rack.

750
01:11:33,838 --> 01:11:43,108
So we can have lots of racks in a data center and they will be side by side, and usually they can be physically secured, so they can be locked,

751
01:11:43,296 --> 01:11:47,620
and only the responsible or the authorized personnel will be able to open that.

752
01:11:48,462 --> 01:11:52,708
If you turn around the rack, so the racks are fixed in aisles,

753
01:11:53,486 --> 01:11:56,804
basically, or rows, and the rows will have aisles in between.

754
01:11:57,326 --> 01:12:05,616
So if you move to the back of a row of racks, you will see the cabling part, and this is how they connect into the network.

755
01:12:05,710 --> 01:12:16,976
So the yellow and green and white that you see here, these are cabling that goes from the servers to the network, and then from the network to the outside world

756
01:12:17,136 --> 01:12:18,820
or wherever they need to communicate with.

757
01:12:25,422 --> 01:12:34,692
The term "on-premises" or "on-prem" is something that you are going to encounter a lot when you deal with cloud or networking or infrastructure and all that.

758
01:12:34,896 --> 01:12:40,644
And this is or refers to a location that is owned and controlled by the customer.

759
01:12:41,710 --> 01:12:49,380
So if we're talking about cloud, AWS, Azure or GCP, these are data centers controlled by these vendors.

760
01:12:50,222 --> 01:12:59,716
But if a company, Bank of America, for example, they have their own data center where they host their servers and applications, so that becomes on-prem for Bank of America.

761
01:13:00,206 --> 01:13:07,076
So when they refer to their own data center, they will talk and say on-prem, or they will mention on-prem on-premises.

762
01:13:07,696 --> 01:13:11,556
That means it's our data center, it's our location, it's our space.

763
01:13:12,846 --> 01:13:15,856
So, what is so special about on-premises data centers?

764
01:13:16,096 --> 01:13:24,096
This is 100% customer controlled, and 100% the customer is responsible for the design.

765
01:13:24,270 --> 01:13:32,176
So they will go out and they will find where is the space? Is it going to be in their building, or maybe they are going to lease it somewhere else?

766
01:13:33,358 --> 01:13:41,572
The cost of establishing the data center, if there are any real estate build or cabling or false ceiling or raised floors,

767
01:13:41,806 --> 01:13:44,164
all of that is the customer responsibility as well.

768
01:13:44,462 --> 01:13:45,616
Who's going to operate that?

769
01:13:45,776 --> 01:13:46,372
The customer.

770
01:13:46,766 --> 01:13:54,052
Who's going to optimize it? Who's going to upgrade it? Who's going to be responsible for the security, the power, the AC, the cooling, and all of that stuff?

771
01:13:54,382 --> 01:13:54,916
The customer.

772
01:13:55,246 --> 01:14:04,576
Of course, the customer may hire companies to do that on the customer's behalf, but still, it is the customer who's responsible to find the third parties and sign

773
01:14:04,736 --> 01:14:13,028
contracts with them and monitor their work to make sure that they are doing an immaculate job as far as the operations of data center are concerned.

774
01:14:13,742 --> 01:14:18,404
So, what is exactly the customer responsible for in a data center?

775
01:14:19,086 --> 01:14:26,116
They are responsible for everything the real estate, the physical security, the cameras, and all that, and then they're responsible for the network.

776
01:14:26,894 --> 01:14:35,396
They are responsible for storage solutions to store the data permanently, a persistent storage for the data.

777
01:14:36,622 --> 01:14:45,572
They are responsible for the hardware, whatever the hardware is, servers, monitors, storage solutions, so on. This is all the customers' responsibility.

778
01:14:45,984 --> 01:14:52,708
Virtualization, if they are going to use virtualization, and definitely in a huge data center virtualization will be used.

779
01:14:53,424 --> 01:15:03,492
Then who is the vendor of the virtualization or the hypervisor and what's the licensing required? What's the cost, the maintenance, support, and all that, they are responsible for that.

780
01:15:03,854 --> 01:15:11,684
They are responsible for deciding on which operating systems are going to be used on the different servers hosting the different applications.

781
01:15:12,462 --> 01:15:19,044
Also, if there are any message brokers, components that have to do between the application components,

782
01:15:19,726 --> 01:15:24,612
basically for them to communicate with one another, they call it middleware or they call it message brokers as well.

783
01:15:25,326 --> 01:15:27,972
They are responsible for deciding what that is going to be.

784
01:15:28,270 --> 01:15:33,744
If it is Python, for example, so what will be the programs that are required to run Python?

785
01:15:33,904 --> 01:15:35,332
We call that the runtime.

786
01:15:35,950 --> 01:15:42,500
And what will be the data or the applications and what will the application communicate and process and store?

787
01:15:42,704 --> 01:15:47,780
And also the actuality or the actual applications is also the responsibility.

788
01:15:48,302 --> 01:15:56,196
So they are responsible for the entire spectrum from the physical all the way to the data and its storage and security and so on.

789
01:15:56,526 --> 01:16:03,504
It's all the customers' responsibility. Who's going to pay for that? It's the customer. And that's why they call it a capex model.

790
01:16:03,886 --> 01:16:09,344
This model, the on-premises data center owned by the customer or leased from another company.

791
01:16:09,744 --> 01:16:14,784
For them, this is called the Capex model, capital expenditure model.

792
01:16:15,184 --> 01:16:18,852
That means, Mr. Customer, you want all of this? Yes, you have to pay.

793
01:16:19,246 --> 01:16:25,584
So you have to pay upfront to buy the equipment, you have to pay up front to rent or own the place.

794
01:16:25,664 --> 01:16:30,436
You have to pay for the construction, you have to pay for the cooling, you have to pay for this, you have to pay for that.

795
01:16:30,766 --> 01:16:33,092
So each time you need something new, okay, fine.

796
01:16:33,390 --> 01:16:36,836
Please show us your credit card and we're going to charge you up front.

797
01:16:38,030 --> 01:16:50,084
To establish and build a data center, it could cost up to $500 million to establish the data center, and then it will be costing a similar cost as well to operate it on an ongoing base.

798
01:16:50,800 --> 01:16:52,864
CapEx model is very expensive to the customers.

799
01:16:52,944 --> 01:17:02,884
Think about a startup, a startup that needs to start and they have an idea and they want three, four, five servers or something, and they don't want to spend a lot of money.

800
01:17:03,534 --> 01:17:09,668
If they use services, they want to pay for it as a utility bill, and if they don't, then they don't pay anything.

801
01:17:10,350 --> 01:17:17,744
As opposed to buying three or four servers and then hosting them in a room in their office, and they don't have office, they are working remote.

802
01:17:17,966 --> 01:17:24,612
So how can they facilitate the power and the cooling and the storage and all that? They have to buy, buy, buy.

803
01:17:25,198 --> 01:17:30,276
So in that case, this is an obstacle and this is a showstopper, they can't afford that.

804
01:17:30,574 --> 01:17:35,812
And this is where the cloud computing comes into play as a utility service

805
01:17:36,544 --> 01:17:40,292
which provides you everything you need and you only pay when you use.

806
01:17:40,782 --> 01:17:42,596
And this will be our next discussion.

807
01:17:46,094 --> 01:17:51,620
So let's look at what cloud computing is compared to on-premises data centers.

808
01:17:51,984 --> 01:18:01,732
And does it really provide us with a financial model other than the CapEx or the capital expenditure which the on-premises data center require?

809
01:18:03,246 --> 01:18:08,064
So, what is the attractiveness of the cloud? And what is the cloud? Why do they call it the cloud?

810
01:18:08,224 --> 01:18:12,864
They call it the cloud because they are going to provide you all the services that you need.

811
01:18:13,294 --> 01:18:18,564
So in cloud computing, everything the customer needs is pre built and ready for use.

812
01:18:19,214 --> 01:18:25,572
So we have everything ready for you. Just decide when you want to use it, enroll or sign up, and then start go ahead and use it.

813
01:18:27,054 --> 01:18:35,140
So that includes the infrastructure, network, physical, storage, all the way to the application and database if you want

814
01:18:35,662 --> 01:18:39,296
passing by storage and security and and and

815
01:18:40,014 --> 01:18:41,252
so all of that is pre-built.

816
01:18:41,710 --> 01:18:48,516
Why do they call it the cloud? Because you don't know where it is, and you shouldn't know, and you are not concerned to know where it is. You need to connect to it,

817
01:18:49,294 --> 01:18:53,540
establish your services, not even knowing where that data center is.

818
01:18:54,862 --> 01:18:57,636
And someone would say, okay, hold on, hold on.

819
01:18:57,966 --> 01:19:00,416
Now you are talking about a data center.

820
01:19:00,590 --> 01:19:03,140
So how is a data center different from the cloud?

821
01:19:04,046 --> 01:19:12,416
In the cloud, the vendor of the cloud, let's say AWS or Microsoft or Google, they have already pre-built the infrastructure.

822
01:19:12,496 --> 01:19:22,756
They have already pre-wired the servers, they put them in the racks, they have the 24-7 operations and they have everything, and it is waiting for customers to come and sign up.

823
01:19:24,174 --> 01:19:27,108
Do you have to go to AWS? No, online, on the internet.

824
01:19:28,176 --> 01:19:33,456
And the promise is what he uses, we'll send you a bill for the usage.

825
01:19:33,614 --> 01:19:38,816
And if you don't use anything, even if you are signed up, we're not going to send you anything. You don't have to pay anything up front.

826
01:19:38,896 --> 01:19:39,588
It's up to you.

827
01:19:40,078 --> 01:19:46,816
If you want in some services to pay a little bit up front to reduce the monthly or the usage bills, that's up to you.

828
01:19:47,022 --> 01:19:49,060
But if you don't want to, that's perfectly fine.

829
01:19:50,286 --> 01:19:56,484
So who has spent money or followed the CapEx model to establish and build that data center? The vendor.

830
01:19:57,230 --> 01:19:59,652
AWS or Azure or GCP.

831
01:20:00,302 --> 01:20:03,652
For you, that becomes an operational expenditure model.

832
01:20:04,398 --> 01:20:06,532
So as you use, you pay.

833
01:20:06,894 --> 01:20:08,548
This is exactly like electricity.

834
01:20:08,976 --> 01:20:16,164
When you travel and you switch off everything in your home and you don't use water or electricity, the bill will be zero.

835
01:20:17,454 --> 01:20:27,364
If you come back and you start using the AC and the water and showers and all that stuff and the bathrooms, then you will be getting some money in the bill next month,

836
01:20:27,616 --> 01:20:27,876
right?

837
01:20:28,558 --> 01:20:29,616
So that's exactly the same.

838
01:20:29,776 --> 01:20:36,576
Like your phone bill, like your utility bill, like your Netflix bill, like your Zoom bill, anything.

839
01:20:36,656 --> 01:20:38,016
When you use, we charge you.

840
01:20:38,176 --> 01:20:45,988
You don't use, we don't charge you. This is called OPEX model or operational expenditure model. And this is where the attractiveness comes from.

841
01:20:46,574 --> 01:20:49,604
Think about the startup we were talking about. They don't have a lot of fund

842
01:20:50,286 --> 01:20:57,536
and they would like to build a proof of concept and they don't want to buy anything, and they would like to switch it off as soon as it is

843
01:20:58,254 --> 01:21:01,348
done and stop paying for anything from that point onwards.

844
01:21:01,774 --> 01:21:06,884
So in that case, OPEX model is perfect because they know what they are going to use.

845
01:21:07,310 --> 01:21:18,496
They will find out about the prices even before using that, and then they can estimate the budget that they'd like to spend, and they have the funds for that little budget, and they start using

846
01:21:18,896 --> 01:21:21,444
finished, switch it off, life is good.

847
01:21:21,710 --> 01:21:28,612
Or build the proof of concept when you go to talk to an investor, turn it on so you can demo that to the investor and then switch it off.

848
01:21:28,736 --> 01:21:29,156
That's it.

849
01:21:29,902 --> 01:21:32,656
So OPEX model is the attractiveness of the cloud.

850
01:21:32,736 --> 01:21:41,572
and that's why when someone tells you that cloud is going to disappear or go away, or people are leaving the cloud and companies are walking away from the cloud.

851
01:21:41,806 --> 01:21:42,416
tell them.

852
01:21:42,638 --> 01:21:54,176
I don't think so, because even when the economic or the global economic outlook is bad, companies would save money by not investing in building data centers

853
01:21:54,336 --> 01:22:00,644
or refreshing data centers or expanding data centers, and they will find cloud solutions to suit them.

854
01:22:00,942 --> 01:22:08,256
So it's easier and faster to build, and also it is cheaper to use in the most case, if they are used properly.

855
01:22:08,416 --> 01:22:15,524
I'm not saying that cloud is cheaper all the way because it turned out in some use cases to be more expensive, not less expensive.

856
01:22:16,974 --> 01:22:25,456
So the message you can respond to those who claim that cloud will go away, that cloud is here to stay, and it will stay because

857
01:22:25,616 --> 01:22:31,588
of the fact that it depends on an OPEX model and customer doesn't have to pay anything up front.

858
01:22:33,998 --> 01:22:45,472
In a lot of the videos that I watch on YouTube and other sources, I'll find a lot of people, especially the ones who are coming from networking background or the ones who teach networking,

859
01:22:45,646 --> 01:22:51,552
Cisco and CCNA, and all that stuff, they always claim that cloud is just a data center.

860
01:22:51,632 --> 01:22:52,036
That's it.

861
01:22:52,590 --> 01:22:54,948
So if you learn networking, you know the cloud.

862
01:22:55,232 --> 01:22:58,180
And that's absolutely, absolutely wrong.

863
01:22:58,926 --> 01:23:08,772
I come from a networking background. I know very well cloud computing as well, and it is absolutely an inaccurate statement to say that the cloud is a data center.

864
01:23:09,422 --> 01:23:16,032
The cloud is built in a data center, but it has hundreds of services that have nothing to do with networking.

865
01:23:16,192 --> 01:23:18,180
Yeah, it functions on a network.

866
01:23:18,510 --> 01:23:27,300
I cannot say that the data center is the street because people use the street to get to the data center, so the data center becomes the street, and that's wrong.

867
01:23:27,918 --> 01:23:29,792
100% the same analogy.

868
01:23:30,112 --> 01:23:32,548
I cannot say the cloud is just a data center.

869
01:23:32,752 --> 01:23:33,188
Why?

870
01:23:33,646 --> 01:23:42,832
Because when we have a customer with the customer's own data center, then I have to go to the data center, I have to get in to start mounting our own servers,

871
01:23:42,992 --> 01:23:45,712
mounting our own racks, connecting them, and all that.

872
01:23:45,952 --> 01:23:51,044
We are building the data center, we have to know where it is, we have to have access to it and all that. That's a data center.

873
01:23:53,390 --> 01:24:02,532
But the data center in the cloud computing is just the component, is just the place, is just the network, but that is not everything in the cloud.

874
01:24:03,214 --> 01:24:07,140
Okay, so it's a component of the cloud, but it's not a synonym of a cloud.

875
01:24:07,534 --> 01:24:09,552
So this is a wrong statement.

876
01:24:09,872 --> 01:24:11,620
Data center is not

877
01:24:12,686 --> 01:24:13,252
the cloud.

878
01:24:13,870 --> 01:24:18,884
It is a component that the cloud uses to provide services to the customers.

879
01:24:20,718 --> 01:24:22,352
So cloud is

880
01:24:23,278 --> 01:24:32,836
the data center housing or hosting the equipment and the servers and the applications and the softwares and the teams and all that for the cloud provider.

881
01:24:33,872 --> 01:24:42,468
But the automation of services in the cloud and the orchestration, basically, when you get to the AWS management console,

882
01:24:43,022 --> 01:24:52,432
you can start to use the different services, including the network, including the storage, including the security, including AI, including machine learning,

883
01:24:52,672 --> 01:24:55,524
including any service. you may think of.

884
01:24:56,032 --> 01:25:08,196
You consume it through a portal or a management console or online basically, and then behind the scenes, whatever you click, it does an action in the background that has been pre-built

885
01:25:08,718 --> 01:25:10,592
to do the task that you have clicked.

886
01:25:10,734 --> 01:25:18,272
So you have the icon representing what you need to do, but then in the background, there's a lot of automation programs that have been written

887
01:25:18,432 --> 01:25:23,620
and scripts that will execute what you are planning to do by clicking that icon.

888
01:25:24,912 --> 01:25:32,992
So the automation and orchestration on top of the data center housing and hosting all the services and servers and applications

889
01:25:33,392 --> 01:25:35,940
that provides me what is a cloud.

890
01:25:36,846 --> 01:25:42,672
So is it enough for me to learn networking and data center to be expert in the cloud?

891
01:25:42,832 --> 01:25:47,972
I will tell you that you are 10-15% of the cloud knowledge that you need to acquire.

892
01:25:48,878 --> 01:25:57,796
So don't fall for those who are telling you you have to start with a networking career, otherwise, you will not be a cloud engineer and you can do cloud without networking.

893
01:25:57,966 --> 01:26:04,592
That is all BS, and we are going to see that in this course. By the end of this course, you can jump into the cloud.

894
01:26:04,814 --> 01:26:09,444
Actually, we are going to start learning the cloud as we learn the fundamentals in this course.

895
01:26:11,534 --> 01:26:21,472
Alright, so again, this building for AWS in Mumbai in India, that's the data center, but there is a lot of programs and scripts, the automation,

896
01:26:21,712 --> 01:26:26,432
the orchestration of services and how they integrate and how they work together.

897
01:26:26,670 --> 01:26:31,792
That is done, and it is provided to you through a web application, a web browser.

898
01:26:32,014 --> 01:26:37,156
You can log in and then you can consume all these services without having to call anyone.

899
01:26:37,552 --> 01:26:44,352
You can deploy servers, you can deploy virtual machines, you can choose the operating systems, the specifications of the virtual machines,

900
01:26:44,432 --> 01:26:53,124
and you can launch the virtual machines and you can work on them without even having to know where that is, and all online and without having to call anyone from AWS.

901
01:26:53,550 --> 01:26:59,492
So that's the beauty of the cloud, that's the attractiveness of the cloud, and that's why the cloud will continue.

902
01:27:03,662 --> 01:27:06,660
Okay, let's now discuss cloud service models.

903
01:27:06,958 --> 01:27:15,748
When I buy a service from the cloud, what is that called? And there are three main ones, and there are plenty of as a service models in the cloud.

904
01:27:17,006 --> 01:27:21,668
Okay, so we'll talk about the as a service or the AAS model in the cloud.

905
01:27:22,126 --> 01:27:26,112
Before we start talking about the cloud, let's think about the on-premises.

906
01:27:26,272 --> 01:27:30,788
What is the customer responsible for and what is the cloud provider responsible for?

907
01:27:31,566 --> 01:27:39,632
So this arrow, if the color is green, that is the span of the customer responsibility, and if it is orange, that will be

908
01:27:39,952 --> 01:27:41,412
the vendor responsibility.

909
01:27:42,286 --> 01:27:48,164
On premises, as we mentioned, the customer is responsible for everything from the network all the way to the application.

910
01:27:50,126 --> 01:27:57,872
And then there is something called IaaS or infrastructure as a service. So basically, you are using infrastructure only from the cloud provider.

911
01:27:57,952 --> 01:28:00,932
For example, you are hosting a virtual machine

912
01:28:01,646 --> 01:28:02,672
in AWS,

913
01:28:03,374 --> 01:28:11,428
and that virtual machine, it's up to you to decide its specs, it's up to you to decide the operating system and which data and what you will use it for, and so on.

914
01:28:12,462 --> 01:28:22,992
So in that case, if it is infrastructure as a service that you are using from the cloud provider, then the cloud provider will be responsible for the network, for the storage, for the hardware,

915
01:28:23,232 --> 01:28:25,412
and the actual virtualization engine.

916
01:28:27,118 --> 01:28:36,516
But once you launch your virtual machine, then choosing the operating system, what middleware, what runtime, what data, what application is your responsibility.

917
01:28:37,230 --> 01:28:40,100
So this is infrastructure as a service.

918
01:28:41,294 --> 01:28:48,032
Now, what if I don't want to be responsible for the operating system and the middleware as well? I want to ship that and the runtime maybe.

919
01:28:48,192 --> 01:28:54,692
I want to have that ready from the cloud providers, and I'm going to take it as part of the service I'm getting from them.

920
01:28:55,438 --> 01:29:04,752
So in that case, the runtime, the middleware and the operating system and everything below is going to be part of the platform as a service or PAS,

921
01:29:05,072 --> 01:29:06,212
PAAS.

922
01:29:07,214 --> 01:29:11,232
In this case, the customer will be only responsible for the application and the data.

923
01:29:11,472 --> 01:29:16,068
Data in the databases and data in the application. We'll talk about databases later on.

924
01:29:17,550 --> 01:29:25,712
The third one, which most of us use right now, like Uber and Netflix and similar services, is called software as a service

925
01:29:25,952 --> 01:29:28,868
or a SaaS offering, SaaS services.

926
01:29:31,182 --> 01:29:37,796
So all of them they have as a service, as a service, as a service, basically like a utility that you are using and you're paying for.

927
01:29:38,222 --> 01:29:48,352
So services in the cloud, majorly they will fall under one of these categories, and that's how we map services to what service models we are using from the cloud.

928
01:29:48,526 --> 01:29:49,796
So these are the three ones.

929
01:29:50,190 --> 01:29:57,796
And of course, when it comes to SaaS, Netflix, and so on, you are not responsible for anything except for signing up and using the service.

930
01:30:01,134 --> 01:30:04,036
Let's talk about cloud deployment models.

931
01:30:04,398 --> 01:30:11,684
Is there anything that prevents a company from building their own orchestration in their own data center and making that a private cloud?

932
01:30:11,982 --> 01:30:18,212
And the answer is there is nothing to prevent them. They can't do that, and that's why there are different deployment models.

933
01:30:18,862 --> 01:30:26,788
So we have public clouds like AWS, Azure, and GCP, and we have private clouds that a bank or a corporate or a hospital

934
01:30:27,312 --> 01:30:37,220
may decide to build in-house and to manage it using their own teams, and that will be private for them only, for that corporate or that organization or that hospital only.

935
01:30:38,030 --> 01:30:42,084
So in public clouds, what is the criteria or the specs?

936
01:30:42,318 --> 01:30:45,316
In public clouds, the resources are publicly shared.

937
01:30:46,062 --> 01:30:55,152
So I could be using a virtual machine on a physical server in AWS, and maybe you are from a different country and you launch a VM and by coincidence

938
01:30:55,312 --> 01:30:58,340
it lands next to my VM on the same physical server.

939
01:30:58,830 --> 01:31:03,172
Public clouds by nature they are shared multi-tenant infrastructures.

940
01:31:04,192 --> 01:31:11,812
And internet connectivity is the common way of getting to your virtual machines and applications in the cloud, in this case,

941
01:31:12,462 --> 01:31:14,916
and it's best suited for less confidential data.

942
01:31:15,598 --> 01:31:24,592
So banks, for example, or hospitals, they will think thrice, not twice, before they are going to move the credit card information for the customers

943
01:31:24,752 --> 01:31:29,476
or their bank account details or their confidential information into the cloud.

944
01:31:30,062 --> 01:31:40,100
And that's why companies they don't live off the public cloud only, but for whatever data that they can't move to the cloud, they can establish their own private cloud.

945
01:31:40,910 --> 01:31:45,252
And as the name implies, the resources available here are going to be for private use.

946
01:31:45,472 --> 01:31:50,532
This is only to host the confidential data that they cannot move into the cloud.

947
01:31:52,238 --> 01:31:58,148
But they can have orchestration and automation in their own on-premises data center, so they have a private cloud.

948
01:31:59,086 --> 01:32:08,800
So this will be for a single customer, and the customer can have can be a group of companies and they are using their private cloud. It doesn't have to be like a one business unit in a company.

949
01:32:08,942 --> 01:32:16,388
It could be multiple business units in Bank of America that are using their private cloud to host different applications and relevant data.

950
01:32:17,678 --> 01:32:22,000
Connectivity in this case, how can they connect into their private cloud?

951
01:32:22,160 --> 01:32:33,924
They can have high-speed connectivity from service providers, they can have their own fiber optic cables that they can provide them hundreds of gigabits per second speeds into that private cloud.

952
01:32:35,822 --> 01:32:39,396
And this is best suited for confidential data client-private systems.

953
01:32:41,006 --> 01:32:41,396
All Right.

954
01:32:41,998 --> 01:32:50,436
What if Bank of America has lots of data that can be moved into the cloud, but also they have sensitive data that they cannot move into the cloud?

955
01:32:50,734 --> 01:32:54,852
So they want to use public cloud, but also they need to use the private cloud.

956
01:32:55,342 --> 01:33:07,364
What if we need communication between the two? For example, an application in the public cloud would like to highlight or reference some data from the private cloud or the other way around.

957
01:33:07,840 --> 01:33:11,492
Then this brings to us what is called a hybrid cloud.

958
01:33:11,950 --> 01:33:20,100
So a hybrid cloud is a mix of public and on-premises private cloud, and both are orchestrated to run a single task.

959
01:33:21,774 --> 01:33:32,080
And that's how it would look like. So the corporate data center with orchestration becomes a private cloud, and then let's say AWS, and they can have private connectivity between them

960
01:33:32,320 --> 01:33:35,300
or connectivity through the internet. Both would work.

961
01:33:37,038 --> 01:33:45,200
And of course, orchestrating this and that, and which users will log in here and which will user will be able to access the cloud, or both of them,

962
01:33:45,360 --> 01:33:46,640
this is orchestration.

963
01:33:46,766 --> 01:33:53,284
It's going to be more complex because now we need to manage private and hybrid access and security and so on.

964
01:33:55,118 --> 01:34:06,080
And this is suitable for so many use cases, and it is proving that hybrid cloud is the direction that most of the companies using the cloud are using right now or they will use.

965
01:34:06,160 --> 01:34:10,244
So that's why we need to be also knowledgeable about hybrid cloud.

966
01:34:12,910 --> 01:34:22,000
And in this case, it's up to the customer to decide what runs here and what runs there, and they can optimize application usage, access, cost optimization as well,

967
01:34:22,240 --> 01:34:26,660
more control, and they can run applications where they decide.

968
01:34:27,054 --> 01:34:39,588
They can use the cloud only for failover when they have a problem in the data center, this called disaster recovery, for example, or they can just store their data as a backup, as a second copy

969
01:34:39,854 --> 01:34:44,804
in the cloud in case if the main data center fails, then we can launch it from the cloud.

970
01:34:47,662 --> 01:34:52,560
Then the trend that is evolving as well, and a lot of companies are deploying.

971
01:34:52,720 --> 01:34:57,828
It has proven that cloud providers are not one size that fits all.

972
01:34:58,574 --> 01:35:03,428
For example, AI and machine learning, Azure and Google are much better than AWS.

973
01:35:03,694 --> 01:35:08,388
So a company might host their AI in Azure or in Google, for example.

974
01:35:09,038 --> 01:35:15,492
Whereas the company will be hosting their e-commerce on AWS because AWS is e-commerce, Amazon is e-commerce.

975
01:35:16,334 --> 01:35:25,680
So now, once they start splitting workloads among the different cloud providers, this also will give them the advantage of avoiding vendor lock-in.

976
01:35:25,998 --> 01:35:34,480
So if I go to AWS and put everything in AWS, now AWS kind of controls me and controls the prices and discounts

977
01:35:34,720 --> 01:35:40,196
because if I'm not happy, I'll have to move stuff and move it elsewhere, which is not an easy task.

978
01:35:40,974 --> 01:35:50,404
And that's why vendor lock-in can be avoided by using multi-cloud and also gives the customer the choice of which one provides me the best features for the lowest cost,

979
01:35:50,720 --> 01:35:52,240
and that is what I'm going to follow.

980
01:35:52,430 --> 01:36:00,960
So multi-cloud is very important, and that's why we always advocate for learning at least two of the three major clouds, if not the three of them.

981
01:36:01,070 --> 01:36:05,188
And I'm not saying you become an expert in all of them, that will be great if you can do it.

982
01:36:05,486 --> 01:36:11,844
But what I'm saying is you need to have enough knowledge about them, not at the start, as you move on into your career.

983
01:36:13,262 --> 01:36:13,600
Alright?

984
01:36:13,998 --> 01:36:23,588
