1174
01:55:17,744 --> 01:55:23,904
When one of the ports is connected and there is traffic on it will be flashing lights showing that this is connected.

1175
01:55:23,984 --> 01:55:33,264
And the light can be green, can be red, can be orange to indicate whether it green means normal operation, orange means there might be some errors,

1176
01:55:33,504 --> 01:55:39,428
red that means a major problem, or maybe it's just switched off, so there is no connection into it.

1177
01:55:40,078 --> 01:55:47,908
And as you can see, this each one of these is called a port or an interface. So this is a switch port where we have the cable connect to.

1178
01:55:49,454 --> 01:55:59,908
So these are 24 ports, that means I can connect physically 24 devices to this switch. So that's an example of how a switch in a LAN would look like.

1179
01:56:00,718 --> 01:56:07,424
Now, if I take that and I start to connect the devices to it, so each one is going to connect through a physical cable.

1180
01:56:07,584 --> 01:56:15,012
We're going to look at the details of the cables later on, and that occupies one port, that occupies a second port, third port, fourth port.

1181
01:56:15,214 --> 01:56:19,556
So now I have 20 ports left for extra devices or for future connectivity.

1182
01:56:20,814 --> 01:56:23,024
So a switch can be physical or virtual.

1183
01:56:23,214 --> 01:56:25,264
So this is a physical switch, we can see it.

1184
01:56:25,424 --> 01:56:30,692
And if it is in front of me, I can touch it as well. I can power it on, I can connect a cable into it.

1185
01:56:30,944 --> 01:56:33,504
But there are also software switches.

1186
01:56:33,664 --> 01:56:36,964
These are the virtual switches, and we're going to cover that as we move on.

1187
01:56:37,806 --> 01:56:42,916
Advantages of switches compared to hubs, it will send data only to the intended device.

1188
01:56:43,182 --> 01:56:53,344
So if we have the same scenario where this laptop would like to send traffic into this printer, the traffic is going to be switched only between this port and this port.

1189
01:56:53,424 --> 01:56:55,844
So this will not see it, and this will not see it.

1190
01:56:56,224 --> 01:57:00,804
Which means I am segmenting the bandwidth. It's not like one common bandwidth.

1191
01:57:01,294 --> 01:57:04,304
So at the same time, this is sending to the printer.

1192
01:57:04,464 --> 01:57:13,668
It could be that someone sitting on this device is displaying the video from the camera on this monitor, for example, and they're completely separate and they don't see each other.

1193
01:57:14,670 --> 01:57:25,344
And it is faster and more secure because now we can do the isolation, it is intelligent as well. We can have security features configured, and each connection can use its full speed.

1194
01:57:25,424 --> 01:57:33,572
If this port is 100 megabits per second or maybe 1 gigabits per second, so it has 1 gig speed into the switch,

1195
01:57:34,146 --> 01:57:35,684
Similarly, the second, third, and fourth.

1196
01:57:37,038 --> 01:57:41,060
And it can handle many devices more efficiently compared to hubs.

1197
01:57:42,318 --> 01:57:49,904
One important feature when we talk about switches is something called virtual local area network or a VLAN.

1198
01:57:50,144 --> 01:57:50,692
What is that?

1199
01:57:51,822 --> 01:57:53,984
So a switch can be configured.

1200
01:57:54,286 --> 01:58:04,004
So we can configure the switch or virtualize the switch to have multiple isolated segments, multiple or completely isolated from one another. They cannot talk to each other.

1201
01:58:05,038 --> 01:58:10,948
So separate segments they are called virtual LANs or VLANs that can be used to group devices logically.

1202
01:58:11,470 --> 01:58:19,332
So as we have discussed the concept of virtualization on a physical server level, that we can have virtual machines into that server,

1203
01:58:19,950 --> 01:58:22,980
we also can have virtual LANs into the switch.

1204
01:58:24,494 --> 01:58:31,232
And how can we do this? We can create VLANs, the different segments. Let's say VLAN 10, VLAN20, VLAN 30, for example.

1205
01:58:31,392 --> 01:58:32,832
That's the ID of the VLAN.

1206
01:58:33,326 --> 01:58:38,180
And then we map the switch port to VLAN 10 that we needed to connect to VLAN 10.

1207
01:58:38,432 --> 01:58:47,172
So the devices that need to connect to VLAN 10 they are going to be configured, or their physical ports on the switch, they are going to be mapped into VLAN 10.

1208
01:58:47,438 --> 01:58:50,500
Similarly for VLAN 20 and 30 and so on. Why would I do this?

1209
01:58:50,926 --> 01:58:58,832
It could be that I need the accountants or the accounting team to be on a separate VLAN and they can communicate with one another and with their server,

1210
01:58:58,992 --> 01:59:09,444
but this is sensitive information. I don't want guests that will connect to the network to be able to communicate or maybe sniff or copy that traffic between the accountants for some reason.

1211
01:59:10,062 --> 01:59:11,472
Okay, and that's how it will look like.

1212
01:59:11,726 --> 01:59:17,952
So now I decided the four leftmost ports are going to be in VLAN.

1213
01:59:18,670 --> 01:59:21,508
I'm just referencing here the color, but it could be VLAN 10.

1214
01:59:22,734 --> 01:59:31,556
And the next four ports are going to be configured or mapped into VLAN, representing it as red, but this could be VLAN 200, for example.

1215
01:59:32,302 --> 01:59:42,192
So any devices that will connect into the red ports or the red map ports will not be able to communicate with the ports that are in the blue or VLAN

1216
01:59:42,352 --> 01:59:49,220
10. They will never be able to communicate this way without extra configuration or extra devices.

1217
01:59:50,510 --> 01:59:51,152
Advantages?

1218
01:59:51,662 --> 01:59:56,432
This is better security. You can have isolation and segmentation into your LAN.

1219
01:59:57,390 --> 02:00:00,752
And isolation also data will only go to the right VLAN.

1220
02:00:00,912 --> 02:00:03,652
So if I send traffic from this device,

1221
02:00:04,430 --> 02:00:13,152
it will only be with the basic configuration of only segmentation, only VLANs, it will only be able to communicate with other ports in the same VLAN.

1222
02:00:13,312 --> 02:00:13,792
And that's it.

1223
02:00:13,952 --> 02:00:20,420
It will never be able to get into the blue port VLANs unless I do something extra.

1224
02:00:22,606 --> 02:00:31,524
Okay, so we talked about the hub and we talked about the switch and we talked about the fact that the switch can be segmented or configured to have isolated segments,

1225
02:00:31,712 --> 02:00:33,028
isolated VLANs.

1226
02:00:33,806 --> 02:00:38,500
Now let's talk about the third device that we may encounter when we are dealing with LANs

1227
02:00:39,310 --> 02:00:47,780
and WANs as well, and that is the router or the router, however you would like to pronounce it. So the router will connect different networks together.

1228
02:00:48,206 --> 02:00:54,532
Networks that are not the same, they will be connected together using a router or a group of routers.

1229
02:00:55,534 --> 02:01:00,036
And these are some examples of physical routers and how they would look like.

1230
02:01:00,718 --> 02:01:09,860
In essence, it looks like switch in terms of ports, but the device is a router. That means it has more intelligence than just a switch.

1231
02:01:10,286 --> 02:01:14,724
And it can have bundled a switching function, but we'll talk about that next.

1232
02:01:16,046 --> 02:01:19,632
So the router will know how to send data from one network to another.

1233
02:01:19,792 --> 02:01:22,756
So that's the connection between separate networks.

1234
02:01:24,718 --> 02:01:29,764
And the router as the switch can be also physical or it can be software virtual.

1235
02:01:31,182 --> 02:01:32,512
And here's an example.

1236
02:01:32,686 --> 02:01:38,512
So we have the branch that was in Germany and the head office that was in the US.

1237
02:01:38,672 --> 02:01:40,740
If I need to connect them, what do I need to do?

1238
02:01:41,454 --> 02:01:46,244
So they will have to connect because they are far away from each other. This is a LAN and this is a LAN.

1239
02:01:46,512 --> 02:01:49,232
They need to connect through routers, through the WAN.

1240
02:01:49,552 --> 02:01:52,132
And the essence of the WAN is routers.

1241
02:01:53,070 --> 02:01:59,332
So I have to procure a router at the Germany branch and another at the US branch.

1242
02:01:59,822 --> 02:02:06,020
I need to connect each one of the routers to the local LAN. It cannot represent the LAN if it is not connected to it.

1243
02:02:06,318 --> 02:02:13,152
Because when traffic will leave one of the devices and needs to get to the US, it has to pass through the router.

1244
02:02:13,232 --> 02:02:21,476
And the only way is to have a physical connection from the router to the same segment or VLAN where these devices connected to.

1245
02:02:22,766 --> 02:02:31,620
Then I need to talk to the service providers, AT &T, for example, or Verizon Wireless or Bell Canada, or whatever the company is.

1246
02:02:31,792 --> 02:02:33,956
and I will ask them: "Okay, here's the address.

1247
02:02:34,190 --> 02:02:41,348
and here's the address in the US. and I would like to connect this address in Berlin or Germany, wherever the city is.

1248
02:02:41,552 --> 02:02:50,212
and I want to connect it to the US. and this is the speed I want. You will sign some contracts, they will take some time, provide the connection, provide the configuration, and everything works.

1249
02:02:51,758 --> 02:02:58,596
So the router can also be used within LAN to connect the different VLANs. Remember when

1250
02:02:59,278 --> 02:03:07,360
we mentioned two slides earlier that a VLAN is a completely isolated segment in a LAN from the other VLANs,

1251
02:03:07,600 --> 02:03:07,840
right?

1252
02:03:08,080 --> 02:03:16,196
What if there is a need for me to have one device from here or maybe the monitor watch the video streams that are coming from the camera?

1253
02:03:16,654 --> 02:03:17,508
How can we do this?

1254
02:03:18,062 --> 02:03:23,268
This is a network and this is a completely separate network. This is a segment and this is a completely separate segment.

1255
02:03:23,534 --> 02:03:31,236
So the only way to do that is to do routing between them because the router is the device that connects multiple LANs together.

1256
02:03:32,014 --> 02:03:33,040
And how can we do this?

1257
02:03:33,280 --> 02:03:39,172
The router must be connected to the different VLANs in order to be able to forward traffic between them.

1258
02:03:39,534 --> 02:03:40,580
And how do we do this?

1259
02:03:40,814 --> 02:03:50,640
I need to have separate interfaces on the router, connect to the different VLANs, and then I will configure the router here to be part of this network and the router

1260
02:03:50,800 --> 02:03:59,620
here to be part of this network, and then the router will be able to forward traffic between the two when there is traffic to be sent across the two networks.

1261
02:04:01,262 --> 02:04:09,200
As I mentioned earlier, there could be some switches that are intelligent and they have routing function or it could be a router that has switching function,

1262
02:04:09,360 --> 02:04:10,468
no matter how it is.

1263
02:04:10,830 --> 02:04:14,404
These are called intelligent switches, or they could be routers with switch functions.

1264
02:04:14,734 --> 02:04:18,532
Sometimes they are referenced as multi-layer switches.

1265
02:04:19,118 --> 02:04:27,120
We are going to talk about why where did the multi-layer come from, but for now, intelligent switch or a switch router combo or a

1266
02:04:27,360 --> 02:04:30,596
multi-layer switch, they are all synonyms for the same thing.

1267
02:04:31,246 --> 02:04:35,428
So the switch router combo device can do both switching and routing functions.

1268
02:04:35,918 --> 02:04:41,252
And we signified the fact that this can do routing as well by the little icon on the switch.

1269
02:04:41,966 --> 02:04:49,188
So in that case, if I need communication between this network and this network, I don't have to have an external router.

1270
02:04:50,126 --> 02:04:56,260
A combo router here, that means there is a software function on the switch that can do routing as well.

1271
02:04:56,686 --> 02:05:02,948
So if I configure that properly, I will be able to allow traffic from the red VLAN into the blue VLAN and the other way around.

1272
02:05:03,630 --> 02:05:08,160
So no need for external routers to connect the different VLANs in this case.

1273
02:05:08,270 --> 02:05:16,228
all right so we learned about hubs, switches, VLANs in a switch, segmenting a switch, router, and now we learn about switch

1274
02:05:16,560 --> 02:05:17,508
router combo.

1275
02:05:17,600 --> 02:05:21,092
And please remember that a router connects different VLANs.

1276
02:05:22,606 --> 02:05:29,220
Not devices in the same VLAN. The switch connects the devices in the same VLAN or it could be multiple VLANs.

1277
02:05:30,080 --> 02:05:35,840
But a router connects the different VLANs or connects a LAN to another LAN through a WAN.

1278
02:05:40,142 --> 02:05:44,320
So we learned about types of networks and network devices that we may encounter.

1279
02:05:44,590 --> 02:05:48,240
So network cabling, how can I wire the router into the switch?

1280
02:05:48,400 --> 02:05:56,868
How can I wire my CCTV camera into the switch? How can I use connectivity in the WAN? What type of cabling, physical cabling that I could use?

1281
02:05:57,550 --> 02:06:04,196
Is it any cable? Can I just bring any wires and connect them? Or does it have to be of specific type?

1282
02:06:04,526 --> 02:06:07,840
And what will be the end connectors that will connect into the devices?

1283
02:06:07,920 --> 02:06:09,572
That's what we need to see right now.

1284
02:06:10,350 --> 02:06:15,396
So network cables are physical wires used to connect computers, routers, switches, and other devices.

1285
02:06:16,014 --> 02:06:23,120
They can carry electrical signals if they are copper cables, or they could be light signals if they are fiber optic cables.

1286
02:06:25,326 --> 02:06:35,684
So let's start with the first one, which is the Ethernet cable or the copper cables. Also, we call it twisted pair or TP cables. They could be UTP, they could be STP, different names for them.

1287
02:06:36,366 --> 02:06:46,404
These cables have standards and they have standard connectors. Maybe you have come across them when you are connecting your TV, for example, to the wall in order to reach the internet.

1288
02:06:47,022 --> 02:06:50,032
So this is called an RJ45 connector.

1289
02:06:50,352 --> 02:06:52,192
RJ45.

1290
02:06:52,272 --> 02:06:58,916
So that's the standard name for this connector. It has eight pins, eight copper pins, and the cable has eight wires.

1291
02:06:59,182 --> 02:07:02,308
They are twisted pairs, and that's why it's called a twisted pair.

1292
02:07:02,926 --> 02:07:06,192
The H2 wires are twisted together in a pair.

1293
02:07:06,382 --> 02:07:12,612
So we have four pairs, and then we have the little pin right here which latches into the switch port.

1294
02:07:13,198 --> 02:07:18,852
So if you look at the pin, and here you will find that there is a groove on the top of the port.

1295
02:07:19,086 --> 02:07:22,308
So there is a wide rectangle and then there is a small rectangle.

1296
02:07:22,734 --> 02:07:33,156
This is where the pin will latch, and the wires on this side are going to touch on wires on this side in the switch, and that's how it connects into the switch.

1297
02:07:35,150 --> 02:07:44,192
So this is used in most homes and offices, short distances, not long distances, and common cable types are Cat5E enhanced Cat

1298
02:07:44,352 --> 02:07:46,564
5 or Cat 6 and Cat 6A.

1299
02:07:47,054 --> 02:07:56,356
Depending on which one, it can go for higher speeds, and the distance is usually limited. We're talking about 100 meters or 90 meters, depending on who you talk to.

1300
02:07:57,198 --> 02:07:59,972
Okay, and it can carry uh speeds from

1301
02:08:00,622 --> 02:08:03,312
100 megabits per second to 10 gigabits per second.

1302
02:08:03,406 --> 02:08:09,668
It could be with the new technologies and all that, could be even higher speeds, but it's usually limited in distance.

1303
02:08:09,966 --> 02:08:15,204
And the data on the cable, the actual traffic is represented as electrical signals.

1304
02:08:15,854 --> 02:08:19,174
So either there is a voltage or there is no voltage. One, zero.

1305
02:08:19,278 --> 02:08:21,956
that That's it. We're going to come into that later on.

1306
02:08:22,798 --> 02:08:30,116
So maximum length is around 100 meters. Some people will tell you 100, some people will tell you 90, but it's very limited distance as we speak.

1307
02:08:32,014 --> 02:08:38,244
The second common cable type that we use in our lands and WANs is the fiber optic cable.

1308
02:08:38,830 --> 02:08:44,484
Fiber optic cables carry light signals and they can go for long distance connections.

1309
02:08:44,974 --> 02:08:53,572
Believe it or not, the internet has underwater, undersea, and under ocean cables to connect the continents together for the internet.

1310
02:08:54,414 --> 02:09:03,792
These cables are sturdy, they are very rugged and very protected, so there is no water leakage and it's not easy for a fish to cut them and all that.

1311
02:09:03,952 --> 02:09:11,652
And when any incident happens, that one of these cables is cut, you will find a lot of countries suffering if they are dependent on that cable.

1312
02:09:12,430 --> 02:09:20,804
So when do we use them? We use them when we need long distances and higher speeds that cannot be achieved using the copper cables or the twisted pair cable.

1313
02:09:21,392 --> 02:09:28,868
And they convey the data, they encode the data, they represent the traffic in light signals and not in electrical signals.

1314
02:09:30,062 --> 02:09:40,352
And they are much faster than Ethernet and can travel longer distances and often used by internet service providers and data centers, and also they are used commonly in WANs,

1315
02:09:40,512 --> 02:09:41,316
wide area networks.

1316
02:09:44,014 --> 02:09:47,780
Now let's talk about data center network or DCN.

1317
02:09:48,398 --> 02:09:56,192
So the data center network, abbreviated DCN, it's a type of a local area network that will connect the servers, storage systems,

1318
02:09:56,352 --> 02:09:59,300
and networking equipment inside a data center.

1319
02:10:00,110 --> 02:10:05,316
So they can communicate and they can serve traffic and they can connect to the internet at a very high speed.

1320
02:10:05,614 --> 02:10:10,672
So it is very common to use fiber cables within a data center and not UTP cables.

1321
02:10:10,798 --> 02:10:18,752
The UTP cables can be used at the end with the servers, that is possible, but fiber is much higher speed, and especially for the important

1322
02:10:18,912 --> 02:10:19,780
servers and so on.

1323
02:10:21,038 --> 02:10:27,972
And as you can see, the data center switch it has similar ports like in the normal switches, but the capacity is much higher.

1324
02:10:28,302 --> 02:10:33,668
The ability to process so the CPU and the memory and all that are much higher as well.

1325
02:10:33,872 --> 02:10:42,468
It usually has multiple power supplies and usually has multiple processor engines as well, so it can have

1326
02:10:42,832 --> 02:10:47,076
more availability, and one failure in a component will not bring the switch

1327
02:10:47,232 --> 02:10:51,044
down. A data center may have lots of these switches.

1328
02:10:51,150 --> 02:10:58,992
and here just representing one switch, but it can have 8, 10, 16, 20, depending on the scale of a data center.

1329
02:10:59,598 --> 02:11:05,444
And the data center usually will have one or more internet connections for diversity and high availability.

1330
02:11:05,806 --> 02:11:14,272
So one or more routers are going to connect to the different switches into the data center, and that's how they can serve traffic to the internet.

1331
02:11:14,638 --> 02:11:21,700
So if I have multiples, that means if one of them dies, the other one is available, and that's called high availability or redundancy.

1332
02:11:23,336 --> 02:11:29,252
all right so we learned about LANs and we learned about data centers. What if I need to connect a LAN

1333
02:11:30,190 --> 02:11:31,072
into a data center?

1334
02:11:31,182 --> 02:11:39,972
So for example, the corporate office exists in a building and adjacent to it we have another building that is the data center of that bank or that corporate.

1335
02:11:40,750 --> 02:11:48,512
So in that case, if the land that I would like to connect is close in a closer vicinity, 10 meters, 20 meters, 1 kilometer,

1336
02:11:48,912 --> 02:11:53,988
500 meters, but not 500 miles away, it is a very close.

1337
02:11:54,670 --> 02:12:02,308
Then what we can do is we can use a fiber cable, we call it an uplink from the switch into the data center switch.

1338
02:12:02,638 --> 02:12:07,792
That is, of course, if this switch is capable of providing fiber optic cables.

1339
02:12:07,872 --> 02:12:14,116
Here we are only representing a port, but if we use copper cables, these are UTP copper ports.

1340
02:12:14,958 --> 02:12:18,692
So if I use a copper cable, that will be only 100 meters away or less.

1341
02:12:19,182 --> 02:12:25,380
But if I want longer distance, 1 kilometer, 2 kilometers, 5 kilometers, then I can use fiber uplinks.

1342
02:12:26,606 --> 02:12:33,156
And of course, I can use multiple ones for redundancy. So if one fails, the other one will still be available.

1343
02:12:33,552 --> 02:12:41,092
And through this uplink, now this LAN can connect or share the internet connection that is available in the data center.

1344
02:12:41,326 --> 02:12:50,500
So that's how we can consolidate one internet connection high speed in the data center, and then all the scattered buildings nearby

1345
02:12:50,752 --> 02:12:57,188
they can use to route or to use the internet instead of having an internet connection out of each building.

1346
02:12:59,438 --> 02:13:05,956
What if the LAN that I need to connect to the data center is far away, it is 200 miles away, 500 miles away, in a different country?

1347
02:13:06,286 --> 02:13:11,684
Then in that case, I have to rely on the WAN wide area network, not the uplinks.

1348
02:13:12,238 --> 02:13:21,828
So in that case, I need to get a router connected to this switch and then talk to the service provider to have a wide area network connection served here

1349
02:13:22,190 --> 02:13:30,404
and another wide area network connection served in the data center, and then proper configuration is done, and that is how they can communicate.

1350
02:13:30,862 --> 02:13:32,032
And of course, the same.

1351
02:13:32,192 --> 02:13:40,752
If I want to have a central internet connection, then through the router, through the other router, the LAN can be using the internet

1352
02:13:40,992 --> 02:13:41,316
connection.

1353
02:13:44,654 --> 02:13:50,884
A corporate or a bank has two big data centers and would like to have some backups between them.

1354
02:13:51,152 --> 02:13:58,884
Copies of the data will be sent between them. So if you lose one, the other one will have the data available or the applications available or the websites available.

1355
02:13:59,502 --> 02:14:01,540
So how can I interconnect two data centers?

1356
02:14:01,632 --> 02:14:02,436
And in that case,

1357
02:14:03,310 --> 02:14:10,724
it depends on whether they are in a close-by vicinity or a far away from each other. But the common one is that we connect them through routers.

1358
02:14:11,214 --> 02:14:15,556
That is more common, although connecting them through switches is possible as well.

