04:50:54,926 --> 04:51:00,132
Okay, so IP routing. How does IP routing work? Very important to understand.

3250
04:51:00,494 --> 04:51:07,364
From what we have learned before, devices on a local area network, if they need to communicate among themselves,

3251
04:51:08,526 --> 04:51:12,388
and as long as they are in the same VLAN, they don't need a router.

3252
04:51:14,638 --> 04:51:22,820
But if any device would like to communicate externally with other sites that don't belong to the same IP network,

3253
04:51:23,760 --> 04:51:26,308
they have to use a router for that.

3254
04:51:26,480 --> 04:51:29,200
Only routers are capable of

3255
04:51:30,126 --> 04:51:31,844
forwarding traffic between networks.

3256
04:51:32,878 --> 04:51:40,960
Also, we learned that if we had two VLANs, red VLAN and a blue VLAN, for instance, and we would like to route between them.

3257
04:51:41,166 --> 04:51:53,348
So in that case, also I need to have a router that connects between them. It will have one leg in the red VLAN, one leg in the blue VLAN, and that router will route between them.

3258
04:51:54,222 --> 04:52:01,604
If the switch has a router or a router functionality, if it is an intelligent switch, sometimes they call it a multi-layer switch.

3259
04:52:01,934 --> 04:52:04,964
So the intelligent switch will have a built-in router

3260
04:52:06,062 --> 04:52:07,780
and it can do that internally.

3261
04:52:08,206 --> 04:52:17,188
But, anyways, going from one subnet to another subnet, if they are in the same LAN but on different networks,

3262
04:52:17,440 --> 04:52:24,080
or if it is from a local network to a remote network through a wide area network, there has to be a router

3263
04:52:25,038 --> 04:52:26,244
used. There is no way around this.

3264
04:52:26,665 --> 04:52:26,905
All right.

3265
04:52:28,302 --> 04:52:34,564
When one device tries to talk to a different network or subnet, it can't go there on its own. It needs a router.

3266
04:52:35,054 --> 04:52:38,820
And the routing is done or the routing is done by devices called routers.

3267
04:52:39,405 --> 04:52:39,620
All right.

3268
04:52:42,030 --> 04:52:48,900
Routing is the process of selecting paths inside a network to forward traffic between hosts.

3269
04:52:49,600 --> 04:52:53,988
So if this camera is trying to send a video footage

3270
04:52:55,118 --> 04:53:05,440
to be saved somewhere in storage in the data center, for example, then it has to forward that traffic to the router because now it's going outside the LAN.

3271
04:53:06,030 --> 04:53:12,580
It will cross a subnet in the WAN and another subnet to get here until it makes it to the storage.

3272
04:53:12,878 --> 04:53:20,292
So it will cross multiple subnets. So it needs a routing decision to be made here. What is the best path to forward this?

3273
04:53:21,422 --> 04:53:25,680
When it gets to this router, this router can send it in or can send it to the internet.

3274
04:53:26,062 --> 04:53:27,040
How would it decide?

3275
04:53:27,280 --> 04:53:31,876
Based on the routing, it will send it inside to the data center because it knows

3276
04:53:32,622 --> 04:53:35,108
that the storage IP address is

3277
04:53:35,950 --> 04:53:38,212
towards the data center switch.

3278
04:53:38,926 --> 04:53:42,160
The data center switch will know where to send it and it will send it in.

3279
04:53:42,286 --> 04:53:46,000
So I have a routing done here, routing done here, and a routing done here.

3280
04:53:46,190 --> 04:53:55,760
Each one of them will do a decision or will make a decision based on its knowledge of where that destination could be

3281
04:53:55,920 --> 04:53:58,244
and the best path to get to that destination.

3282
04:53:58,798 --> 04:54:03,120
In order to do that decision, the routers will use what we call routing tables.

3283
04:54:03,310 --> 04:54:08,772
Sometimes they are called forwarding database, forwarding tables, and algorithms

3284
04:54:09,614 --> 04:54:13,796
in order to determine the most efficient path to forward traffic.

3285
04:54:14,510 --> 04:54:25,060
So if it has if the router has multiple ways to get to the data center on the other side, then which one to use? Then there has to be an algorithm that will enable the router to find the best path.

3286
04:54:26,030 --> 04:54:36,132
When thinking about the routing tables or the forwarding database, the best analogy is think as if we are now configuring a GPS

3287
04:54:37,120 --> 04:54:40,640
on the router to define directions to destinations.

3288
04:54:40,720 --> 04:54:45,060
So the routing table is exactly like a map or a GPS on the router.

3289
04:54:47,214 --> 04:54:53,476
Each router needs to build a routing table or a database of known destinations and how to reach them.

3290
04:54:55,406 --> 04:54:59,044
When a router receives an IP packet, it will follow the process below.

3291
04:54:59,408 --> 04:55:03,908
Based on the existing routing table or the forwarding database on the router,

3292
04:55:05,230 --> 04:55:11,940
when the packet is received into one of the router ports, one of the router interface, let's say this one is connected to a switch.

3293
04:55:13,198 --> 04:55:13,636
Alright?

3294
04:55:13,998 --> 04:55:21,156
And then we have a PC on the switch or a laptop that is trying to send something outside the network so it gets to the router.

3295
04:55:21,966 --> 04:55:23,940
So that message is called a packet.

3296
04:55:24,288 --> 04:55:27,888
That message from the laptop to the router, each one is called a packet.

3297
04:55:28,014 --> 04:55:29,124
So packet is received.

3298
04:55:29,806 --> 04:55:35,300
The router will receive the packet, it will check who is the sender and where is the destination.

3299
04:55:36,558 --> 04:55:42,308
Now it would look at the destination and then check the GPS, check the routing table that it has.

3300
04:55:43,022 --> 04:55:45,444
Do I know where that destination is?

3301
04:55:45,742 --> 04:55:47,204
If the answer is yes,

3302
04:55:49,646 --> 04:55:55,300
where should I forward it? And then it will send it to the IP address or the interface.

3303
04:55:55,982 --> 04:56:09,488
So here for example, destination 10.0.0.0 slash 24. If you want to get to it, send it to the IP address 100.2.1.1, which is a connected router or a connected network.

3304
04:56:11,182 --> 04:56:15,328
If you don't know where it is, then by all means, drop it.

3305
04:56:15,502 --> 04:56:16,128
Don't guess.

3306
04:56:16,334 --> 04:56:17,700
The router will never guess.

3307
04:56:18,318 --> 04:56:22,608
If it knows how to get to the destination, it will send it towards the destination.

3308
04:56:22,768 --> 04:56:24,836
If it doesn't, it will just drop it.

3309
04:56:25,230 --> 04:56:29,444
And that's it. And you will not get an error telling you that I have dropped it because of such and such.

3310
04:56:29,966 --> 04:56:38,448
You will be waiting on your browser trying to get to that website that is hosted, and things are not working because you haven't configured your router

3311
04:56:38,688 --> 04:56:38,948
properly.

3312
04:56:42,542 --> 04:56:44,928
Routing tables, who is going to be building them?

3313
04:56:45,088 --> 04:56:49,488
Who is responsible to populate that database or the GPS?

3314
04:56:49,568 --> 04:56:52,132
Who is responsible to put the data inside it? So

3315
04:56:52,848 --> 04:56:59,012
the routing table or the forwarding database can be configured manually, and this is called static routing.

3316
04:56:59,854 --> 04:57:08,848
If your infrastructure is simple, small, you can do static routing, and that means you are configuring the routing manually and you are building the router

3317
04:57:09,008 --> 04:57:10,116
database manually.

3318
04:57:11,214 --> 04:57:15,428
That is an option when we are talking about a small scale infrastructure.

3319
04:57:15,854 --> 04:57:21,508
And we are going to do manual configurations by the way in our demos in AWS.

3320
04:57:22,734 --> 04:57:25,540
If it is larger than that, if I have two routers,

3321
04:57:26,894 --> 04:57:35,684
all right, and let's say this router knows about other five route or three routers, and this one knows about another four routers,

3322
04:57:36,654 --> 04:57:39,808
and each one of these routers knows about multiple routers.

3323
04:57:40,048 --> 04:57:43,524
I have so many networks now. I mean, this could be like a mini-internet.

3324
04:57:44,302 --> 04:57:52,688
It doesn't make sense to go ahead and build the information in each one of these routers to let it know the possible

3325
04:57:53,008 --> 04:57:56,128
destinations and how to get to them. It doesn't make sense.

3326
04:57:56,288 --> 04:58:00,196
You will not be able to maintain it from an operational perspective, it will be a nightmare.

3327
04:58:01,102 --> 04:58:03,876
And also it will be prone to mistakes. So, what do we do then?

3328
04:58:04,430 --> 04:58:08,900
There is something called routing protocols, dynamic routing protocols.

3329
04:58:09,248 --> 04:58:14,608
For example, border gateway protocol is the dynamic routing protocol used on the internet.

3330
04:58:15,008 --> 04:58:17,604
Any routers on the internet they communicate,

3331
04:58:18,350 --> 04:58:28,804
especially the big ones, the backbone ones, they communicate using BGP because the internet table size could be in the multiple gigabytes, if not more, than that.

3332
04:58:30,158 --> 04:58:31,040
So, what do they do?

3333
04:58:31,150 --> 04:58:42,720
We enable BGP here, we enable BGP here, we enable BGP on the routers, and then the routers, each one of them, will start to share the networks that it knows about with all the neighbors.

3334
04:58:43,406 --> 04:58:52,164
So this will will also share the information, and that's how they start to populate their GPS, the routing database, the routing table, the forwarding database

3335
04:58:52,400 --> 04:58:53,092
dynamically.

3336
04:58:53,440 --> 04:59:02,980
Either way, a router must have a forwarding database, an accurate one, either manual or dynamic, in order to be able to forward traffic properly.

3337
04:59:03,374 --> 04:59:06,564
The router does not come pre-configured with a root table.

3338
04:59:07,150 --> 04:59:16,100
The router comes from the manufacturer and then you place it in your infrastructure and you have to configure it and customize it according to your infrastructure needs.

3339
04:59:16,622 --> 04:59:18,564
So this is something that you will need to do.

3340
04:59:19,182 --> 04:59:21,840
So requires dynamic routing protocols to be configured.

3341
04:59:22,094 --> 04:59:27,172
Open shortest path first is an example, and border gateway protocol is another example.

3342
04:59:27,534 --> 04:59:35,600
When you work with AWS, you will need to know about BGP if you would like to scale connectivity and configure wide

3343
04:59:35,760 --> 04:59:42,692
area network and enable dynamic routing protocols and all that. But you can live with a static if that is sufficient.

3344
04:59:42,887 --> 04:59:52,000
all right so the routing table will be populated either dynamically or statically manually, and then the flow chart

3345
04:59:52,240 --> 04:59:55,332
or the decision making process will work.

3346
04:59:55,918 --> 05:00:00,132
Okay, there is a concept called default route or default route.

3347
05:00:00,400 --> 05:00:00,996
What is that?

3348
05:00:02,254 --> 05:00:04,484
In the previous examples, we looked at

3349
05:00:05,134 --> 05:00:05,440
this

3350
05:00:06,638 --> 05:00:09,668
forwarding table, and the decision was

3351
05:00:10,830 --> 05:00:13,956
either I know where the destination is so I can forward it.

3352
05:00:14,160 --> 05:00:17,348
That's the router decision based on its forwarding table

3353
05:00:18,094 --> 05:00:18,884
or root table.

3354
05:00:19,214 --> 05:00:20,836
And if it doesn't know, it will drop it.

3355
05:00:21,520 --> 05:00:23,200
This is not practical.

3356
05:00:24,302 --> 05:00:25,892
Let me tell you why.

3357
05:00:26,734 --> 05:00:31,920
Let's say, for example, this router is the one connected to the big I to the internet.

3358
05:00:34,640 --> 05:00:44,612
And this is the LAN that will send the traffic or maybe the wireless LAN in your office that is connected to the router, and then the router has this table,

3359
05:00:46,382 --> 05:00:48,720
and based on that, it will forward to the internet.

3360
05:00:49,966 --> 05:00:58,116
So when it receives your message, it will look at where it is supposed to go and then will check if it has an entry for that destination,

3361
05:00:58,670 --> 05:00:59,940
and then it will send it out.

3362
05:01:00,174 --> 05:01:01,220
But this is not practical.

3363
05:01:02,446 --> 05:01:07,748
On the internet, we have millions and millions and millions of networks and websites.

3364
05:01:08,110 --> 05:01:11,040
Who is going to configure all of this here?

3365
05:01:11,342 --> 05:01:12,708
I mean, it's my home office.

3366
05:01:13,646 --> 05:01:21,380
I'm not going to run BGP as the dynamic routing protocol on my small router because it doesn't support it. It cannot process it.

3367
05:01:21,710 --> 05:01:28,356
It cannot maintain the full internet table that requires gigs and gigs and gigs of storage on the router.

3368
05:01:28,974 --> 05:01:31,588
So this mini router cannot store it, the wireless router.

3369
05:01:33,710 --> 05:01:43,172
And at the same time, I'm not going to go on the router and start configuring the 10 million websites that I may

3370
05:01:44,302 --> 05:01:48,036
reach out to or try to connect to throughout my life.

3371
05:01:48,590 --> 05:01:49,412
Nobody does that.

3372
05:01:49,998 --> 05:01:50,960
So what's the alternative?

3373
05:01:51,120 --> 05:02:00,132
To avoid having to fill manually a large routing table, and even if you try, you will not be able to make it because there are millions and millions of networks.

3374
05:02:01,840 --> 05:02:14,052
And if you want to run that dynamically, the router does not support the dynamic routing protocols. And even your ISP will not provide you any BGP information because you are just a home user.

3375
05:02:15,374 --> 05:02:23,520
They would do that if they are connecting to a university, if they are connecting to a corporate like IBM or one of the big companies, they would give you the full internet

3376
05:02:23,760 --> 05:02:24,320
table.

3377
05:02:24,590 --> 05:02:28,516
But for you as a home user, of course not, and your router cannot support it.

3378
05:02:29,294 --> 05:02:33,988
Way out of this is we use something called the default route.

3379
05:02:34,510 --> 05:02:37,764
And we are going to stretch the flowchart a little bit.

3380
05:02:40,110 --> 05:02:47,972
A default route is basically to tell the router if you don't have a specific entry for this destination, please send it this way.

3381
05:02:48,686 --> 05:02:57,072
So it defines a path to the router in case if it doesn't have an explicit entry in its root table for a specific destination,

3382
05:02:57,358 --> 05:03:00,548
how to deal with it. Don't drop it, please do this instead.

3383
05:03:01,870 --> 05:03:09,732
So default route is a special route table entry that can be used as a last resort, a last choice to forward a packet.

3384
05:03:10,512 --> 05:03:16,388
Only if only if there is no other specific entry in the route table to be used.

3385
05:03:17,166 --> 05:03:19,392
So this is like your plan B.

3386
05:03:19,552 --> 05:03:25,412
If you can't find a matching entry in the route table, please go for plan B and use this path.

3387
05:03:26,766 --> 05:03:28,260
So how would that look like?

3388
05:03:28,718 --> 05:03:33,988
So in a route table, there will be an entry like this, and sometimes it's written as slash zero.

3389
05:03:34,830 --> 05:03:38,992
And that means to the router, here are the specific entries.

3390
05:03:39,152 --> 05:03:44,164
If you get anything going to an IP address within this range, please use this entry.

3391
05:03:45,710 --> 05:03:51,524
If you have anything headed into this range as a destination, then use this entry.

3392
05:03:52,430 --> 05:04:00,512
If you get a destination IP address in a packet, so it is going to an IP address that does not belong here and does not belong here,

3393
05:04:00,782 --> 05:04:01,732
do not drop it.

3394
05:04:02,478 --> 05:04:05,252
Instead, forward it to this IP address.

3395
05:04:07,438 --> 05:04:10,660
And the flowchart would be a packet is now received.

3396
05:04:11,214 --> 05:04:14,032
Is the destination known through a specific entry?

3397
05:04:14,192 --> 05:04:16,932
If yes, go ahead and forward it according to the entry.

3398
05:04:17,870 --> 05:04:19,364
If no, do not drop it.

3399
05:04:20,174 --> 05:04:24,612
Is there a default route, a gateway of last resort in the table?

3400
05:04:25,486 --> 05:04:28,836
If the answer is no, then go ahead and drop it. There is nothing you can do.

3401
05:04:29,614 --> 05:04:34,276
But if yes, then please forward it according to the details in the entry.

3402
05:04:34,798 --> 05:04:41,668
So this is very important and it helps save effort and minimize the size of the root table immensely.

3403
05:04:42,574 --> 05:04:46,512
In your home office, you have your own local network that doesn't need the router.

3404
05:04:46,832 --> 05:04:49,872
But then if you would like to go to the internet,

3405
05:04:51,152 --> 05:04:56,804
then the router should send it to the ISP through the link, and the ISP should deal with it, right?

3406
05:04:57,102 --> 05:05:03,588
So you have only way out to any destination. So in that case, on that router, it's enough to have only the default route.

3407
05:05:04,270 --> 05:05:09,092
0.0.0.0 send it on this interface to the ISP and let them deal with it.

3408
05:05:09,678 --> 05:05:10,912
Hopefully, this is understood.

3409
05:05:10,992 --> 05:05:21,472
We are going to look at an example of how we populate that and we include the default routes as well in order to cement the knowledge and clarify any ambiguities.

