3649
05:24:59,374 --> 05:25:07,428
If you do this, if you have your workload or application or virtual machines distributed across more than one availability zone,

3650
05:25:07,712 --> 05:25:12,804
then in the eyes of AWS, you are highly available. Your workload is highly available.

3651
05:25:13,070 --> 05:25:22,820
Alright, so that's enough about the AWS global infrastructure. Next, we're going to introduce what is an AWS VPC, the virtual private cloud in an AWS region.

3652
05:25:26,446 --> 05:25:39,364
So let's now go ahead and discuss what is an AWS VPC. A virtual private cloud that is your virtual data center that you can create in a minute in cloud computing or in AWS cloud to be more specific.

3653
05:25:42,638 --> 05:25:49,316
So a virtual private cloud building your own isolated virtual data center in the cloud.

3654
05:25:49,966 --> 05:25:55,940
So the VPC is a virtual data center in the cloud which is isolated from other VPCs by default.

3655
05:25:57,454 --> 05:26:08,708
The VPC client or the customer who owns the VPC who created the VPC has full control over the VPC or the VPCs. You can create more than one if you want.

3656
05:26:09,870 --> 05:26:13,124
And a VPC is confined to a single AWS region.

3657
05:26:13,232 --> 05:26:17,232
You will never see a VPC stretching between two regions.

3658
05:26:17,392 --> 05:26:24,356
It's either in region A or B or C, but it's not going to be half in here and the other half in another region.

3659
05:26:28,846 --> 05:26:35,492
A default VPC is created automatically in each AWS region when an AWS account is created.

3660
05:26:38,030 --> 05:26:39,172
And the VPC

3661
05:26:39,872 --> 05:26:42,432
spans all the availability zones in a region.

3662
05:26:42,592 --> 05:26:43,472
What does that mean?

3663
05:26:43,758 --> 05:26:48,260
What does it mean when I say that a VPC stretches across availability zone? That means

3664
05:26:48,910 --> 05:26:53,828
in that VPC or virtual data center, you can have virtual machines and workloads.

3665
05:26:54,670 --> 05:27:05,232
We call the applications and the data that you add and all that a workload or an application or a database, whatever that is, you can host it in any of the availability zones in that region.

3666
05:27:05,392 --> 05:27:13,952
As if we are saying that you can control where in the region to place your workload inside the VPC.

3667
05:27:14,032 --> 05:27:14,788
It's up to you.

3668
05:27:15,152 --> 05:27:24,036
You can have a virtual machine for application A in availability zone A and in availability zone 2 or B and in availability zone C.

3669
05:27:24,782 --> 05:27:27,908
You can have databases in availability zone A only.

3670
05:27:28,878 --> 05:27:29,636
That's up to you.

3671
05:27:29,902 --> 05:27:30,756
You can have

3672
05:27:31,502 --> 05:27:38,404
some security virtual machines also available in availability zone two and three only, but not in one.

3673
05:27:38,798 --> 05:27:39,524
It's up to you.

3674
05:27:40,142 --> 05:27:49,508
Creating a VPC means you have the permission to create a workload, a virtual machine anywhere in the region in any of the availability zones.

3675
05:27:50,272 --> 05:27:56,548
Why is that an advantage? Because it allows you to have more availability of your applications.

3676
05:27:56,752 --> 05:28:05,872
I can now have them or have my e-commerce website in two availability zones in the region. If one gets busted for whatever reason, I have the other one.

3677
05:28:05,952 --> 05:28:14,692
And remember, we mentioned that the availability zones are far away from each other, and each availability zone is a cluster of one or more data centers

3678
05:28:17,646 --> 05:28:21,668
The availability zones themselves are also interconnected with very high speed.

3679
05:28:22,222 --> 05:28:31,236
So hosting your application or data or website in more than one availability zone gives you an advantage of being more available

3680
05:28:31,662 --> 05:28:35,716
and the uptime will be higher and your customers are going to be happier.

3681
05:28:38,382 --> 05:28:45,348
So, what are the components of a VPC? When I build a virtual data center that takes me a minute or less, as you will see.

3682
05:28:46,062 --> 05:28:49,380
What are the components that I can create in a VPC?

3683
05:28:49,934 --> 05:28:53,072
First of all, you can't have a VPC without a cider block.

3684
05:28:53,152 --> 05:28:58,340
There has to be an IP address block of which you can divide subnets

3685
05:28:59,182 --> 05:29:00,036
for the VPC.

3686
05:29:01,006 --> 05:29:04,068
And there is a router configured automatically

3687
05:29:05,070 --> 05:29:12,036
and it connects to all the subnets that you create within a VPC and it guarantees that they can all talk to each other.

3688
05:29:13,326 --> 05:29:22,532
Then you have the root tables, which will tell the implied router for each subnet what to do with the traffic coming into the subnet or going out of the subnet. We're going to discuss that in detail.

3689
05:29:23,278 --> 05:29:30,404
If the VPC needs to connect to the internet, that only happens through the internet gateway or abbreviated the IGW.

3690
05:29:31,470 --> 05:29:35,428
Now for security, we are going to discuss that in subsequent modules.

3691
05:29:35,822 --> 05:29:42,564
We have security groups and network access control lists, which are virtual firewalls, but we'll discuss them when we get to them.

3692
05:29:45,006 --> 05:29:46,660
So let's start with a cider block.

3693
05:29:48,112 --> 05:29:51,396
You cannot have a VPC without a cider block.

3694
05:29:51,632 --> 05:29:56,516
It's all IP communication inside the VPC, so there has to be an IP address range.

3695
05:29:58,286 --> 05:30:01,028
And the VPC subnets are created from that

3696
05:30:01,870 --> 05:30:02,512
cider block.

3697
05:30:02,702 --> 05:30:08,036
So that's the extra large pizza out of which you can break down slices which are going to be your subnets.

3698
05:30:09,262 --> 05:30:17,872
And the cider block size can be anywhere from as small as slash /28 to as big as slash 16.

3699
05:30:18,032 --> 05:30:23,332
So this one gives you a total hosts of 65,000 plus.

3700
05:30:24,462 --> 05:30:26,884
And this one gives you only 16 hosts.

3701
05:30:27,392 --> 05:30:35,140
Of course, this is not something that you would want to do unless if you are testing something for 5 minutes, 10 minutes, half an hour, and then you will switch it off.

3702
05:30:36,014 --> 05:30:40,004
Default size for cider blocks in AWS are slash 16.

3703
05:30:40,270 --> 05:30:42,432
Can I have slash 15, slash 14?

3704
05:30:42,512 --> 05:30:43,652
No, that's not allowed.

3705
05:30:44,261 --> 05:30:53,220
All right, so the VPC stretches across all availability zones in one region, and the VPC is confined within one region, cannot stretch beyond the region.

3706
05:30:53,646 --> 05:30:57,508
And I can have one or more cider blocks actually, but at least one.

3707
05:30:58,112 --> 05:30:59,428
And the subnets

3708
05:31:00,752 --> 05:31:05,956
are chunks out of that cider block, and they can be in different availability zones.

3709
05:31:09,198 --> 05:31:14,308
Let's now discuss subnets and root tables as far as a VPC goes.

3710
05:31:16,078 --> 05:31:17,444
So let's start with subnets.

3711
05:31:19,054 --> 05:31:21,796
We can configure multiple subnets per VPC.

3712
05:31:22,576 --> 05:31:31,172
As big as your original size block of IP addresses, the CIDR block, you can create as many subnets as you want.

3713
05:31:33,550 --> 05:31:42,016
So I can have one subnet in availability zone one, one subnet in availability zone two, and I can have more than one subnet per availability

3714
05:31:42,176 --> 05:31:42,820
zone if I want.

3715
05:31:44,334 --> 05:31:49,764
As we mentioned that the VPC is confined within a region, cannot stretch beyond a region boundary.

3716
05:31:50,798 --> 05:31:55,556
Similarly, a subnet cannot stretch outside of an availability zone.

3717
05:31:56,046 --> 05:32:01,376
So the subnet and all its hosts must be within the same availability

3718
05:32:01,376 --> 05:32:06,276
zone. So the VPC is confined to a region, the subnet is confined to an availability zone.

3719
05:32:06,496 --> 05:32:14,148
So can I configure a virtual machine here and assign an IP address on subnet 1 in availability zone 2? The answer is no.

3720
05:32:15,022 --> 05:32:21,380
If subnet 1 is in availability zone 1, all the hosts in that subnet must be in availability zone 1 as well.

3721
05:32:22,670 --> 05:32:25,376
Can I have more than one subnet per availability zone?

3722
05:32:25,456 --> 05:32:26,340
By all means, yes.

3723
05:32:28,334 --> 05:32:31,296
So now we have the subnets, but we need the implied router.

3724
05:32:31,536 --> 05:32:41,028
You don't need to configure it. It will be created automatically and it will connect to every single subnet that you created or will create in the future in your VPC.

3725
05:32:42,094 --> 05:32:48,260
So nothing needs to be done on that implied router except for, if you want, create root tables.

3726
05:32:48,976 --> 05:33:00,964
The implied router will forward traffic among the subnets in a VPC, but it also will have the traffic or will help the traffic forward the traffic that is going outside the VPC

3727
05:33:01,582 --> 05:33:05,316
or traffic coming from the outside to one of the subnets in the VPC.

3728
05:33:05,870 --> 05:33:12,068
So basically, this is where traffic can roam around within the VPC, go out, or

3729
05:33:12,846 --> 05:33:15,332
traffic can be received through the implied router.

3730
05:33:18,606 --> 05:33:22,816
Routing between subnets within a VPC is enabled by default and you cannot disable it.

3731
05:33:22,931 --> 05:33:23,908
we are going to look at that.

3732
05:33:26,254 --> 05:33:34,628
The implied router by default will have one routing table, and the routing table would say all the subnets within the CIDR block

3733
05:33:34,896 --> 05:33:37,060
are local. I know how to get to them all.

3734
05:33:38,062 --> 05:33:42,404
So all the routing among the subnets is enabled by default, and you don't need to do anything about it.

3735
05:33:44,782 --> 05:33:51,844
This router is fully managed by AWS. You cannot access it, you cannot log into the router, and you cannot change configuration in the router.

3736
05:33:51,936 --> 05:33:55,588
All you can do is you can play with the root tables that are available to you.

3737
05:34:00,878 --> 05:34:05,764
Root tables, as I mentioned, each VPC has a default root table, it's called the main root table.

3738
05:34:06,382 --> 05:34:10,016
That root table can be used by more than one subnet at the same time.

3739
05:34:10,096 --> 05:34:15,812
So if I decide to keep only one root table in the VPC, all the subnets in the VPC are going to use it.

3740
05:34:17,294 --> 05:34:23,236
The router can hold a separate root table if required for each subnet.

3741
05:34:24,494 --> 05:34:26,724
So I can have RT1, RT2, RT3.

3742
05:34:27,406 --> 05:34:33,668
Or you can have one root table and all the subnets are going to use it. So the router will use the same root table for all the subnets.

3743
05:34:34,350 --> 05:34:36,016
Is that in your control? 100%.

3744
05:34:36,430 --> 05:34:42,980
You can create custom root tables as required and you can assign them to the subnets that you want. And we are going to do this.

3745
05:34:45,806 --> 05:34:49,376
The subnet can attach to only one root table at a time.

3746
05:34:49,536 --> 05:34:54,336
So you can switch the association of a root table from one subnet to another.

3747
05:34:54,574 --> 05:35:03,588
So let's say this subnet now I have it with the green root table. I can decide to switch it to the gray root table or to the blue root table.

3748
05:35:04,110 --> 05:35:07,076
But at any given moment it will have only one root table.

3749
05:35:07,456 --> 05:35:11,076
That means when traffic is going into the router from the subnet,

3750
05:35:12,014 --> 05:35:20,164
the router will consult the root table for that subnet and find out how to forward it. And we're going to do that in the demo as well.

3751
05:35:23,566 --> 05:35:30,148
Alright, so how can we provide internet access to the VPC through what we call an internet gateway?

3752
05:35:30,766 --> 05:35:34,692
So the internet gateway is how a VPC can communicate with the internet.

3753
05:35:35,758 --> 05:35:38,276
So I have a VPC with subnet and implied router.

3754
05:35:38,608 --> 05:35:39,556
I have to add

3755
05:35:40,448 --> 05:35:44,964
the internet gateway, otherwise, the subnet cannot get out to the internet.

3756
05:35:45,646 --> 05:35:49,124
And I can have only one internet gateway per VPC.

3757
05:35:49,870 --> 05:35:53,188
So it is fully managed service, you cannot access it and log on to it.

3758
05:35:53,646 --> 05:35:57,568
And there is only one that you can create and attach to the VPC at a time.

3759
05:35:57,728 --> 05:36:06,660
I can remove it and attach another one, but this is a fully managed, scalable, highly available service from AWS, you don't need to worry about that.

3760
05:36:08,750 --> 05:36:17,476
In order for a subnet to use the internet gateway, its routing table must have an entry pointing to the internet gateway, like this, for example.

3761
05:36:17,888 --> 05:36:23,204
Any IP address not specifically listed in the root table, please send it to the IGW.

