06:16:48,750 --> 06:16:54,244
The router connects this local area network, the mini local area network we have on the drawing,

4273
06:16:55,246 --> 06:16:57,060
and connects that to the internet.

4274
06:16:57,710 --> 06:17:05,920
The local area network has this range 172.31.1.024.1 is configured on the router

4275
06:17:06,240 --> 06:17:07,140
LAN interface

4276
06:17:08,046 --> 06:17:09,828
and the router is connected to the switch.

4277
06:17:10,190 --> 06:17:16,196
And we have a laptop that has an IP address 172.31.1.118.

4278
06:17:17,006 --> 06:17:24,868
Both belong to the same network and everything looks good, and they are both configured on ports that belong to the default VLAN

4279
06:17:25,422 --> 06:17:28,480
so they can communicate with one another.

4280
06:17:28,910 --> 06:17:32,804
And this laptop is configured to send anything

4281
06:17:33,582 --> 06:17:40,004
that is going outside the network to send it through to the default gateway or.1.1.

4282
06:17:40,910 --> 06:17:42,560
This is all easy.

4283
06:17:43,630 --> 06:17:47,012
Now the user of the laptop would like to get to CNN.com.

4284
06:17:47,534 --> 06:17:55,236
And CNN.com happens to have the IP address on the internet 205.111.37.86.

4285
06:17:56,558 --> 06:18:00,228
And both are connected to the internet. Connections are live and life is good.

4286
06:18:00,814 --> 06:18:11,300
The router got an IP address from the service provider, a public IP address on the One side on the internet side, which is 100.2.55.33.

4287
06:18:12,238 --> 06:18:16,740
The browser needs to formulate a packet that will go to CNN.com.

4288
06:18:18,446 --> 06:18:19,172
So the packet,

4289
06:18:20,720 --> 06:18:31,588
assuming just for analogy, that it is an envelope, so the message is or the payload is put inside an envelope, and now the browser is going to write,

4290
06:18:32,078 --> 06:18:36,804
I am the sender 172.31.1.118, which is a private IP.

4291
06:18:37,518 --> 06:18:42,756
And this should go to CNN.com, which is 205.111.37.86.

4292
06:18:44,558 --> 06:18:50,052
And it will send it to the router because this is going outside the network, so this will need to be sent to the router.

4293
06:18:50,926 --> 06:18:54,404
The router will receive it, will look at the source IP address,

4294
06:18:55,054 --> 06:19:00,260
and finds out that this is going on the internet, and the router understands this cannot happen.

4295
06:19:00,942 --> 06:19:01,760
So what do we do?

4296
06:19:02,080 --> 06:19:05,092
NAT will kick in network address translation.

4297
06:19:05,582 --> 06:19:11,364
And what the router will do is it will remove the private IP address from the message

4298
06:19:12,206 --> 06:19:16,164
and it will replace it with its own public IP address.

4299
06:19:18,350 --> 06:19:20,388
And the destination does not change.

4300
06:19:20,720 --> 06:19:25,700
Now the message can be sent through the internet and it will arrive safely at CNN.com.

4301
06:19:26,446 --> 06:19:34,212
CNN.com will receive the message, read what is required. So that user wanted to go into the financial news

4302
06:19:34,990 --> 06:19:35,520
page.

4303
06:19:35,840 --> 06:19:40,240
The response is going to be formulated, put in an envelope,

4304
06:19:40,640 --> 06:19:44,612
and the sender in this case is going to be the server itself,

4305
06:19:45,518 --> 06:19:50,436
and the receiver or the recipient is going to be the public IP address of

4306
06:19:51,182 --> 06:19:51,748
the router.

4307
06:19:52,480 --> 06:19:53,840
How and why?

4308
06:19:54,160 --> 06:19:57,760
Because when it received the message, this is what appeared to be the

4309
06:19:57,760 --> 06:20:00,960
source. So now it gets to the router.

4310
06:20:01,294 --> 06:20:05,764
The router has a table of mapping of what has been done.

4311
06:20:06,414 --> 06:20:12,356
And part of that was that I have taken the 172.31.1.118

4312
06:20:13,230 --> 06:20:17,892
and I have swapped it with my 100.2.55.33.

4313
06:20:18,446 --> 06:20:25,520
So now when this comes back, it's going to do the reverse process, but it will not remove the source.

4314
06:20:25,600 --> 06:20:36,804
It will remove the destination, the one that has been before placed as the source, and swap it with the private IP address, and that is how it gets it to the laptop.

4315
06:20:37,838 --> 06:20:42,564
So this is how it works, and exactly the same happens at home in our wireless routers.

4316
06:20:44,718 --> 06:20:55,876
So if we use the same example, and assuming the router received this IP address from the service provider, of course it could be a completely different one, but it's a public IP address.

4317
06:20:56,848 --> 06:21:03,408
We have this range for the network within your home, which is being broadcast by the router on the wireless LAN.

4318
06:21:04,078 --> 06:21:12,196
The router has the.1.254 on the WLAN on the wireless LAN side, and this has the.1.120.

4319
06:21:12,368 --> 06:21:13,764
The exact same thing repeats.

4320
06:21:14,766 --> 06:21:19,908
The private IP address is the source when a message is being sent from the laptop to the server,

4321
06:21:20,974 --> 06:21:30,404
gets to the wireless router, the wireless router will stop it, will change this IP address with this IP address and then send it to the recipient

4322
06:21:30,768 --> 06:21:31,492
on the internet.

4323
06:21:32,430 --> 06:21:34,660
And the response is the reverse process.

4324
06:21:35,662 --> 06:21:44,644
In AWS, when you have a virtual machine that has a public IP address, the public IP address is not configured on the instance.

4325
06:21:45,422 --> 06:21:49,572
It will be configured on the IGW, on the internet gateway.

4326
06:21:50,862 --> 06:21:57,828
And mapping will happen between the private IP address of the instance and its assigned public IP address.

4327
06:21:59,246 --> 06:22:02,608
And we are going to see how that happens in the hands-on map.

4328
06:22:02,688 --> 06:22:02,980
Alright?

4329
06:22:03,214 --> 06:22:09,732
So, network address translation is extremely crucial to conserve the public IP address space

4330
06:22:10,688 --> 06:22:19,364
by masking private IP addresses and allowing the use of private IP addresses internally within wireless LANs or local area networks,

4331
06:22:19,968 --> 06:22:25,380
and converting that into public IP addresses. when a communication is required with the internet.

4332
06:22:25,998 --> 06:22:29,728
And it does the reverse process on the response traffic.

4333
06:22:30,030 --> 06:22:37,008
Hopefully it is clear, it's very easy and it happens, and you are using it at home all the time without you knowing.

4334
06:22:37,168 --> 06:22:40,516
So this is how you are seen on the internet as a public IP address.

4335
06:22:40,688 --> 06:22:45,284
And that is how on the internet, when someone attacks

4336
06:22:46,350 --> 06:22:54,564
a website or a critical system or something, they look at the logs and they find out who was the source IP address.

4337
06:22:55,054 --> 06:23:06,308
And then wherever that is, they can find quickly the country, which service provider, and from the service provider logs, they can find out who was using that IP address at the time of the attack,

4338
06:23:06,574 --> 06:23:12,548
and that's how they can track you down to your home and find you when you have done something wrong on the internet.

4339
06:23:12,974 --> 06:23:18,788
Alright, so be careful when you use the internet, use it as a good citizen and don't use it as a bad citizen.

