07:03:04,928 --> 07:03:14,528
So please use your imagination that there are components in the software and hardware in the laptop that are going to do these functions. Which component?

4806
07:03:14,688 --> 07:03:15,172
Who cares?

4807
07:03:16,206 --> 07:03:27,364
You are not the software developer right now, you are just starting. Maybe later on in the future, you will understand this very well and you will start developing to these different functions.

4808
07:03:28,270 --> 07:03:37,220
But for now, these functions exist in every one of our devices, even your your phone, iPhone or Android, it does have all of that.

4809
07:03:39,342 --> 07:03:46,756
This is the IP stack or the TCP IP stack inside your laptop or phone or iPad that will be doing this. Okay.

4810
07:03:47,246 --> 07:03:50,628
Now the transport layer needs to add headers.

4811
07:03:50,768 --> 07:03:56,068
Why? Because it is the transport from here to there to the other side.

4812
07:03:56,526 --> 07:03:57,796
Is it directly? No.

4813
07:03:58,128 --> 07:04:01,860
But it has to add headers so the other side will understand.

4814
07:04:02,190 --> 07:04:04,100
Do we need to open a connection or not?

4815
07:04:04,750 --> 07:04:09,328
Do we need to follow the sequence of each packet? So if one is missed, then I need to tell you.

4816
07:04:09,488 --> 07:04:13,988
Do we need to have flow control? So if you are sending too much, I need to slow you down.

4817
07:04:14,478 --> 07:04:21,636
Do we need to have extra communication basically to ensure that there is nothing missed, nothing is received in error or out of sequence?

4818
07:04:21,888 --> 07:04:25,348
And if anything is missed, then we can talk and you can resend it again.

4819
07:04:25,966 --> 07:04:29,808
If we need to do that, or just I will send you whatever I will send, receive it.

4820
07:04:29,998 --> 07:04:31,556
And if we miss anything, who cares?

4821
07:04:32,398 --> 07:04:36,356
And both of these are valid options when it gets to a transport layer.

4822
07:04:36,910 --> 07:04:46,048
So that transport is going to add its own headers, the bits and pieces, and we'll deliver it. Okay, now it's time to ship it through the network.

4823
07:04:46,128 --> 07:04:47,088
So what do we need to do?

4824
07:04:47,342 --> 07:04:55,556
We need to give it to the layer that understands routing and understands IP addresses and understands BGP, for example, if that's required, and other stuff.

4825
07:04:56,608 --> 07:04:59,268
network address translation also happens at this layer.

4826
07:04:59,822 --> 07:05:01,008
Do we need to add headers?

4827
07:05:01,168 --> 07:05:01,700
Of course.

4828
07:05:02,190 --> 07:05:05,380
That header exists, and here's our original data.

4829
07:05:05,902 --> 07:05:07,488
And now an IP header is added.

4830
07:05:07,648 --> 07:05:11,844
Source, destination, IP addresses are added, and if anything else is needed.

4831
07:05:12,238 --> 07:05:17,508
At the transport, this is the source port and destination port will be there as well.

4832
07:05:19,088 --> 07:05:20,516
Goes through the switch.

4833
07:05:20,846 --> 07:05:31,140
For the switch to deliver to the different ones in the same local area network, then probably headers are going to be added also. These are called data link or link layer or link to headers.

4834
07:05:32,750 --> 07:05:40,688
Once this is done, it is ready now to go out on the next card to the network, to the local area network, and then possibly to the another

4835
07:05:40,928 --> 07:05:42,052
router or something.

4836
07:05:43,758 --> 07:05:53,348
So it will filter down to now if you are holding your iPhone connected through Wi-Fi, then this has to be converted into frequencies in the wireless LAN.

4837
07:05:54,734 --> 07:06:03,568
If this was a wired copper port into a switch, then it will be converted into electrical signals to be sent through the copper wire.

4838
07:06:03,648 --> 07:06:09,636
If it was optical cable, fiber optic cable, then it will be converted into light signals to be sent.

4839
07:06:09,902 --> 07:06:12,036
Who is going to do that? That is the physical layer.

4840
07:06:13,742 --> 07:06:20,836
So this path from the source from the application until it gets to the physical layer is called encapsulation.

4841
07:06:21,134 --> 07:06:30,368
Why is that? Because we have the data and imagine how many times we have added things around it as if it is a capsule at the end. We are encapsulating the data.

4842
07:06:30,448 --> 07:06:34,596
We are including the data but adding a lot of headers around it.

4843
07:06:35,694 --> 07:06:43,972
Now it gets as a series of binary zeros and ones, either presented as electrical signals or light signals or however.

4844
07:06:44,590 --> 07:06:51,172
It gets to the other side. The physical layer here, it needs to do the reverse process de-encapsulation.

4845
07:06:52,302 --> 07:06:59,808
So the physical layer here is going to translate that into what is acceptable for the data link layer.

4846
07:06:59,888 --> 07:07:08,772
So the data link layer will be able to read any information required for the data link and comprehend it and find out if it is for me or for someone else,

4847
07:07:09,168 --> 07:07:16,288
then it's going to remove layer two headers and it's going to pass it on to the network layer.

4848
07:07:16,448 --> 07:07:21,088
The network layer will look at the source and destination IP where this is going.

4849
07:07:21,326 --> 07:07:23,328
If it is to me, I will process it else.

4850
07:07:23,408 --> 07:07:25,604
If I'm a router, then I'm going to send it.

4851
07:07:26,158 --> 07:07:31,620
If it is a router, it will end at this layer and it will not have a lot of information above.

4852
07:07:33,774 --> 07:07:46,052
Now, if this was the server as we are showing here, then it will pass it on to the transport layer to understand. Okay, was that supposed to be a connection-oriented communication or connectionless?

4853
07:07:46,926 --> 07:07:51,248
Do we need to look at the sequencing and all that or not?

4854
07:07:51,438 --> 07:07:54,468
Do we need to do retransmission or not if something is missing?

4855
07:07:54,894 --> 07:08:02,180
And it understands the protocol or the port it's going to. Is it going to HTTP 80, for example, or port 80?

4856
07:08:02,382 --> 07:08:10,848
If it is going to port 80, then it will remove each one after it does the processing, it will remove the header and will leave the up

4857
07:08:11,022 --> 07:08:12,740
the headers for the layers above.

4858
07:08:13,518 --> 07:08:16,448
If this was a router, then the router will pass it on.

4859
07:08:16,782 --> 07:08:25,168
So we'll process the network layer, we'll look at any filtering required, and then it will pass it on to the next router or the other or to the server at the end. But this is not our case.

4860
07:08:25,328 --> 07:08:32,516
We're using a simple case that they are both close to each other. There are no hubs or no devices in between.

4861
07:08:33,326 --> 07:08:40,996
So now it goes up, session layer reads the information, presentation layer, and then it goes to the application layer.

4862
07:08:41,328 --> 07:08:49,764
Now the application here will understand it, and then the application will become when it for the response, it will become the sender,

4863
07:08:50,062 --> 07:08:51,408
and here will be the receiver.

4864
07:08:51,502 --> 07:09:01,648
So as if we are going to reverse the functions, encapsulation for the response, and then de-encapsulation at the laptop level for the response.

4865
07:09:01,838 --> 07:09:03,236
So that's exactly how it happens.

4866
07:09:03,598 --> 07:09:12,740
If we didn't have all the standards and we did modularize the different functionalities, the TCP IP stack inside any device would have been a nightmare.

4867
07:09:13,166 --> 07:09:23,588
But now, since we have broken these into functions and standards and all that, it's easy for a company, for example, to specialize in only doing the scrambling function of data

4868
07:09:24,750 --> 07:09:32,288
or only a deployment of a new protocol at the transport layer, for example, or a new function at the transport layer.

4869
07:09:32,526 --> 07:09:39,204
So they can focus or zoom into a specific functionality and develop for that functionality only.

4870
07:09:41,134 --> 07:09:49,060
So the source software will take the data from the application, flows down through the different layers to the physical layer. This is called encapsulation.

4871
07:09:49,488 --> 07:09:54,528
And then at the receiving end, we do the opposite, which is called de encapsulation.

4872
07:09:54,608 --> 07:09:59,940
From the physical up is de encapsulation, from the application down is encapsulation.

