Here we are ignoring the IP addresses, but of course, let's say this is IPS

5710
08:23:49,134 --> 08:23:50,660
or IPC for the client.

5711
08:23:51,696 --> 08:23:53,764
And this is IPS.

5712
08:23:54,606 --> 08:24:01,716
So this could be 200.10.1.5, and this could be 150, for example, .2.1.1.

5713
08:24:02,926 --> 08:24:04,068
So we'll focus on the ports.

5714
08:24:04,238 --> 08:24:08,228
So the source port randomly selected from this range unused in the client.

5715
08:24:08,654 --> 08:24:11,684
And the destination is going to be 22 TCP.

5716
08:24:12,686 --> 08:24:15,044
And this is the traffic from the client to the server.

5717
08:24:15,822 --> 08:24:20,736
So the server is going to receive it, check on the key and check on all the information, and now it is good to go.

5718
08:24:20,942 --> 08:24:29,508
So what the server would do, it will respond and it would say, This is being sent from the source, which is the SSH server, port 22,

5719
08:24:30,096 --> 08:24:33,188
going back to what used to be the source in the request.

5720
08:24:34,382 --> 08:24:37,540
And of course, the IPs are going to be IPC,

5721
08:24:38,336 --> 08:24:44,816
in this case, going to IPS, which is the server IP address, and in this direction they are going to be reversed.

5722
08:24:44,910 --> 08:24:47,556
The source will be IPS and the destination will be IPC.

5723
08:24:49,582 --> 08:24:55,428
Connection established, the administrator does whatever he or she needed to do, and now it's time to test it.

5724
08:24:56,270 --> 08:24:58,948
So they need to do an HTTP connection.

5725
08:25:00,942 --> 08:25:06,116
connection is open but they open the browser and now they need to go and do HTTP into the server

5726
08:25:07,214 --> 08:25:13,380
So again, the software here will look at this dynamic range and see what has been already taken.

5727
08:25:14,318 --> 08:25:17,220
For example, 61234 will never be picked up.

5728
08:25:18,094 --> 08:25:23,364
It will pick up another one that is unused. And here we have more than enough 16,000 plus

5729
08:25:24,334 --> 08:25:27,556
ports available to be used as a source port.

5730
08:25:27,776 --> 08:25:33,892
And I don't think any of us will have 16,000 sessions open at the same time

5731
08:25:34,574 --> 08:25:41,508
to 16,000 different applications and websites and all that. Maybe have 50, 100, 200, but never 16,000.

5732
08:25:42,478 --> 08:25:48,548
So it will decide on this is free, the 50,000, and the destination in this case will be TCP 80.

5733
08:25:48,896 --> 08:25:51,748
Why? Because we know that the server is listening on this port.

5734
08:25:52,686 --> 08:25:57,936
Server will receive it, understand it because it's standard HTTP, and now it's time to respond.

5735
08:25:58,158 --> 08:26:00,068
So when it responds, they will be reversed.

5736
08:26:00,256 --> 08:26:04,096
The source is going to be 80 and the destination is going to be 50,000.

5737
08:26:04,302 --> 08:26:12,388
So please, please, please stick this fact about the source port and a destination port in forward direction

5738
08:26:12,622 --> 08:26:14,692
and they will be reversed in the other direction.

5739
08:26:14,990 --> 08:26:16,804
Now let's expand it a little bit.

5740
08:26:17,102 --> 08:26:22,884
Let's say that this server would like to go on the internet to an update server,

5741
08:26:25,070 --> 08:26:29,732
and the update server is going to be using port 443, for example.

5742
08:26:30,542 --> 08:26:31,428
So HTTPS.

5743
08:26:34,510 --> 08:26:40,032
Alright, so the request is going to be initiated from the server out.

5744
08:26:40,192 --> 08:26:43,684
So the destination is going to be port 443 in this case,

5745
08:26:44,814 --> 08:26:49,284
while the source is going to be for a random port selected, depending on how the software will do it.

5746
08:26:54,606 --> 08:27:00,964
Alright, let's now discuss the security or the cybersecurity introduction and the network security introduction.

5747
08:27:02,350 --> 08:27:07,908
A lot of people get confused about network security and cybersecurity, and they think they are the same thing.

5748
08:27:08,366 --> 08:27:15,140
And here we are going to clarify the relation between network security and cybersecurity for beginners.

5749
08:27:16,046 --> 08:27:24,272
In this module, we're going to introduce what cybersecurity is and what exactly it covers, and then we are going to look at network security.

5750
08:27:24,352 --> 08:27:29,412
So the focus is going to be about network security, but we have to take the 50,000 feet view first.

5751
08:27:30,254 --> 08:27:35,268
Look at what cybersecurity is and the role of network security within cybersecurity.

5752
08:27:35,950 --> 08:27:38,532
We are going to extend the discussion to network security.

5753
08:27:38,926 --> 08:27:46,788
We're going to talk about network devices again, but this time we're going to focus on the firewalls, the very famous or the most

5754
08:27:47,312 --> 08:27:53,700
common device in protecting or in securing your network and the data within.

5755
08:27:54,062 --> 08:28:05,188
We'll look at firewall examples and we are going to use two examples from AWS: the security groups and the network ACLs, and both of them are layer four firewalls.

5756
08:28:05,472 --> 08:28:06,404
What does that mean?

5757
08:28:07,712 --> 08:28:16,740
That means they have the intelligence to understand up to the transport layer only, but they don't go beyond that. They don't understand what's happening in the application.

5758
08:28:17,072 --> 08:28:22,948
They cannot comprehend any headers that are added by HTTP or by encryption.

5759
08:28:23,470 --> 08:28:28,516
So they only understand IP addresses and ports, and that's what they can permit or deny based on.

5760
08:28:28,846 --> 08:28:30,884
And we will also look at encryption.

5761
08:28:31,310 --> 08:28:39,620
Then we're going to look at virtual private networks, which relates very well to encryption that we have mentioned multiple times, but now it's time to explain what that is.

5762
08:28:43,982 --> 08:28:47,172
So let's go ahead and start with cyber security overview.

5763
08:28:47,630 --> 08:28:49,312
What is cybersecurity basically?

5764
08:28:49,518 --> 08:28:57,892
Before we do that, we'll take a step back and we'll do an analogy so you understand the importance of security when it comes to networks and data.

5765
08:28:58,542 --> 08:29:01,732
So think of your phone or computer like your house.

5766
08:29:02,032 --> 08:29:03,684
What do you do to secure your house?

5767
08:29:04,270 --> 08:29:07,428
There has to be a lot of different ways to secure it.

5768
08:29:08,590 --> 08:29:11,332
There are the passwords that we need to protect and firewalls.

5769
08:29:11,726 --> 08:29:15,232
So let's say, for example, this is locking your doors every

5770
08:29:15,232 --> 08:29:20,432
night. And then we have the scrambling of the data so nobody can read your data.

5771
08:29:20,512 --> 08:29:26,884
This is like when you spread or close your curtains so nobody can see you in the evening while you are inside the house.

5772
08:29:28,752 --> 08:29:30,592
Then we have an alarm system.

5773
08:29:30,862 --> 08:29:35,552
If anyone breaks into a window or breaks into a door, then we need that.

5774
08:29:35,712 --> 08:29:40,420
So that is the role in cybersecurity for antiviruses and security monitoring.

5775
08:29:41,166 --> 08:29:49,156
Then probably you will have a device with a camera and mic that will check who's at the door before opening the door, right?

5776
08:29:49,614 --> 08:29:52,352
So think like authentication in networks.

5777
08:29:52,494 --> 08:29:53,860
So we are going to cover all of this.

5778
08:29:54,032 --> 08:29:56,644
So these are stuff that cybersecurity covers.

5779
08:29:56,912 --> 08:29:59,556
So cybersecurity is about protecting computers.

5780
08:30:00,174 --> 08:30:09,392
Computers include servers, include storage, include anything that does computations, protecting networks, so nobody can break into the network and bring it down.

5781
08:30:09,712 --> 08:30:11,396
Delete the configuration, for example.

5782
08:30:11,950 --> 08:30:17,988
And also the data, whether it is stored or travels across the network from being stolen,

5783
08:30:18,638 --> 08:30:20,964
attacked, like hacked, or damaged.

5784
08:30:21,454 --> 08:30:26,308
So this is the core responsibility of cybersecurity in general.

5785
08:30:26,672 --> 08:30:32,964
So please note that we talked about networks and we talked about computers, but this is part of what you need to protect.

5786
08:30:34,126 --> 08:30:35,744
Why is cybersecurity important?

5787
08:30:35,984 --> 08:30:42,628
Because it will protect your personal information, your credit cards, your photos, your money, your bank accounts, your passwords.

5788
08:30:43,024 --> 08:30:46,692
That is all done by implementing cybersecurity measures.

5789
08:30:47,278 --> 08:30:56,228
Also, to stop hackers from breaking into systems. Whether these are websites, they will hack or even your laptop and then they will ask you for a ransom.

5790
08:30:56,686 --> 08:31:03,264
And also to keep companies and services running. Some hackers or types of the hacks is the denial of service.

5791
08:31:03,504 --> 08:31:13,892
They will have loads and loads of traffic on a server such that the resources on the server, the memory and CPU will be busy and they will not be able to serve legitimate users.

5792
08:31:13,984 --> 08:31:22,020
So this is also type of attacks that people can do in order to deny the service from legitimate users.

5793
08:31:22,766 --> 08:31:27,012
So this can happen in banks, in hospitals and in schools, universities, and so on.

5794
08:31:27,598 --> 08:31:30,144
Also to prevent data loss or leaks.

5795
08:31:30,478 --> 08:31:39,264
So cybersecurity is very wide in its responsibility to protect a lot of things and to make sure that your IT is healthy, is operational

5796
08:31:39,424 --> 08:31:44,464
the right way, and is protected from any malicious activities.

5797
08:31:46,318 --> 08:31:48,996
So cybersecurity is not network security.

5798
08:31:49,262 --> 08:31:56,224
Network security is part of cybersecurity. So let's look at the areas or specializations that are under cybersecurity.

5799
08:31:56,384 --> 08:31:58,596
What are they and what are these specializations?

5800
08:31:59,214 --> 08:32:03,716
So we look at network security. This is one of many areas under cybersecurity.

5801
08:32:03,904 --> 08:32:12,228
And as the name implies, this is about protecting network devices and protecting the data stored or the data that is moving

5802
08:32:12,624 --> 08:32:17,184
over these networks or through the internet or local area networks.

5803
08:32:17,774 --> 08:32:26,544
Then we have application security, which is the security of the software and applications from bugs, from backdoors, from Trojans,

5804
08:32:26,798 --> 08:32:32,356
from from from there are so many things that you can do to protect applications and software.

5805
08:32:32,944 --> 08:32:39,504
Then we have the endpoint security. I need to have security software on your laptop, for example.

5806
08:32:39,758 --> 08:32:43,184
I need to have encryption, for example, done on your laptop.

5807
08:32:43,264 --> 08:32:53,380
I need to have similar things to detect malicious activities on servers to make sure that they are sending updates and notifications about their performance and all that

5808
08:32:53,614 --> 08:32:55,140
to monitoring and alerting.

5809
08:32:55,598 --> 08:32:58,884
So this is for devices like server storage, laptops, and phones.

5810
08:32:59,054 --> 08:33:04,260
They have to have endpoint security, and there are companies that are specialized in endpoint security.

5811
08:33:04,494 --> 08:33:05,824
And then we have cloud security.

5812
08:33:05,934 --> 08:33:09,316
So it could be the network that you own, or it could be the cloud.

5813
08:33:09,646 --> 08:33:19,716
And in that case, in the cloud, then the provider of the cloud will be responsible for the network, and you will be responsible maybe for the endpoint security and the application security and so on.

5814
08:33:20,064 --> 08:33:25,664
So infrastructure and data in the cloud must be protected as well. There is identity and access management.

5815
08:33:25,904 --> 08:33:30,724
Who can access your infrastructure, whether it is in the cloud or on-prem?

5816
08:33:31,504 --> 08:33:35,584
And when they access it, what are they allowed to do?

5817
08:33:35,886 --> 08:33:38,628
Are they allowed to get into databases and change things?

5818
08:33:38,864 --> 08:33:43,844
Or is it only read-only to look at the configurations and find out if it's done right or wrong?

5819
08:33:44,384 --> 08:33:55,584
Can they change configurations? Can they delete resources? Or they are just high-level entry-level users, they can just view and don't do all of that. So that is all in identity and access management.

5820
08:33:55,664 --> 08:33:56,452
Who are the users

5821
08:33:57,774 --> 08:33:59,940
that are allowed to do this and at what level?

5822
08:34:00,398 --> 08:34:05,184
So is allowed to access what and what they can do. And then there is the physical security itself.

5823
08:34:06,030 --> 08:34:08,644
AWS has data centers, Azure has data centers.

5824
08:34:09,104 --> 08:34:12,324
Now, who is allowed to get into these data centers?

5825
08:34:12,784 --> 08:34:15,300
And what is happening in the data centers?

5826
08:34:15,584 --> 08:34:26,624
And who went in at what time and where did they walk within the data center? So even if they are allowed, then we look at badges that will allow them in specific areas and not anywhere.

5827
08:34:26,704 --> 08:34:37,380
They cannot just go ahead and open any cabinets or any cage and walk in if it belongs to third parties or different companies, or if it is an area where they should not be in.

5828
08:34:40,942 --> 08:34:42,692
And then policies and rules.

5829
08:34:43,054 --> 08:34:46,912
For security and security policies and all that, there are rules and policies.

5830
08:34:47,118 --> 08:34:54,276
What are the standards implemented for that? And this is also part of cybersecurity, which is the compliance and auditing part.

5831
08:34:54,672 --> 08:35:01,028
So as you can see, network security is just one of maybe seven or eight different areas in cybersecurity.

5832
08:35:01,678 --> 08:35:12,432
So please be aware in your career that nobody is going to fool you intentionally or based on ignorance and tell you that cybersecurity is network security. It's the same thing.

5833
08:35:12,592 --> 08:35:17,348
You need to take network plus and CCNA before you do cybersecurity.

5834
08:35:17,712 --> 08:35:19,620
And my answer to that would be

5835
08:35:20,622 --> 08:35:23,524
what if I'm going to specialize in these areas?

5836
08:35:23,712 --> 08:35:27,632
Why do I have to dive deep into network security?

5837
08:35:27,792 --> 08:35:30,500
Maybe cloud security will have a little bit of that as well.

5838
08:35:30,894 --> 08:35:35,108
So if I'm not specializing in this one or that one, why should I

5839
08:35:36,046 --> 08:35:37,472
dive deep into networking?

5840
08:35:37,806 --> 08:35:44,996
If I'm going to do endpoint security or physical security or compliance and auditing, why do I need to be the expert of networking?

5841
08:35:45,742 --> 08:35:56,804
So please be aware of what cybersecurity is and what network security is and how they relate to one another. This is very important to keep in mind as you cruise through your IT career.

5842
08:36:00,622 --> 08:36:03,844
Alright, so now let's focus on network security.

5843
08:36:04,032 --> 08:36:11,332
The normal extension or the normal continuation is to go through network security as a branch of cybersecurity.

5844
08:36:11,472 --> 08:36:14,148
So please keep that in mind all the time.

5845
08:36:14,958 --> 08:36:19,524
So network security, it is about protecting the network devices and data.

5846
08:36:20,272 --> 08:36:27,364
As the data travel across or live on a network, get stored on a network in a database or in a storage device.

5847
08:36:27,982 --> 08:36:39,152
The mission of the network security is to keep the bad guys out and make sure that the data is safe and only the good guys should be logged in to the network or access

5848
08:36:39,312 --> 08:36:41,860
the network and only do what they are allowed to do.

5849
08:36:43,214 --> 08:36:54,912
So that will take us to discuss network devices again, but now we are going to focus on the security part. What are network security device, The very common one that we will face day in and day out.

5850
08:36:55,072 --> 08:37:02,980
Whether we are using AWS or Azure or Google Cloud or even if you are working on premises, we always need to think about firewalls.

5851
08:37:03,854 --> 08:37:09,312
Firewall is a security gate, is a security guard of your network and applications.

5852
08:37:09,486 --> 08:37:15,552
So it can protect the network and it can protect the servers or the computing devices and the applications on

5853
08:37:15,552 --> 08:37:22,980
top. Is it going to do everything in the application? The answer is depending on the intelligence of the device.

5854
08:37:23,472 --> 08:37:33,632
Does it delete the need for end point security? And the answer is no. They are integral parts of the overall cybersecurity mechanism that you do.

5855
08:37:33,934 --> 08:37:36,832
So here's an example of a firewall that we can place.

5856
08:37:36,992 --> 08:37:44,032
It will protect the local area network switch and it will protect the application servers and database servers if we are in a data center.

5857
08:37:44,192 --> 08:37:52,964
Of course, some people would place that in front of the router. That's a possibility if the firewall is an advanced one that can also process routing.

5858
08:37:54,542 --> 08:38:01,828
The firewall can be configured to allow or sometimes it's called deny or block data coming in.

5859
08:38:02,350 --> 08:38:07,588
If data is coming into the network or the server, this is called inbound.

5860
08:38:08,206 --> 08:38:12,900
If data is being sent out of the network, this is called outbound.

5861
08:38:13,454 --> 08:38:17,828
So some people would call it ingress for inbound and egress for outbound.

5862
08:38:17,952 --> 08:38:23,748
So please understand what this means because they are very important when you are configuring your firewalls.

5863
08:38:25,774 --> 08:38:37,616
Once configured, the firewall will be like this security guard that is going to be looking around and trying to figure out if there is anything that's not supposed to run through the firewall,

5864
08:38:37,696 --> 08:38:40,932
then it will stop it, depending on the configuration that you have placed.

5865
08:38:41,582 --> 08:38:50,576
When I was stressing before on the importance of understanding the flows and the ports, and what is the source port and what's the destination port, what's the source IP,

5866
08:38:50,736 --> 08:38:52,256
what's the destination IP.

5867
08:38:52,416 --> 08:39:02,980
Because if you don't understand it well, you will configure the firewall in a wrong way, and that will deny legitimate traffic and may allow traffic that is not supposed to run through the firewall.

5868
08:39:04,942 --> 08:39:08,416
Firewalls can protect at the network level or at the application level.

5869
08:39:08,686 --> 08:39:16,388
So firewalls might be a layer 4 firewall, which means it understands IP and transport layer only. That's called a layer 4 firewall.

5870
08:39:17,038 --> 08:39:25,456
An advanced firewall, sometimes it's called next generation firewall or web application firewall, that is a firewall that can understand up to layer 7.

5871
08:39:25,616 --> 08:39:31,044
So it can process all the headers and it can even look at the payload at layer 7.

5872
08:39:31,566 --> 08:39:39,268
The complexity of the firewall depending on the budget and depends depends on also the use case, what do you need to use it for.

5873
08:39:41,006 --> 08:39:48,656
So we might also deploy separate firewalls at the separate segments, and it depends on which business you are in.

5874
08:39:48,878 --> 08:39:54,576
So there are some standards depending on the business that they have to comply with, and part of that compliance,

5875
08:39:54,736 --> 08:40:00,580
there will be some closes about firewalling in your infrastructure and how many firewalls have to be there.

5876
08:40:02,158 --> 08:40:11,332
So we could also have hardware firewalls or software-based firewalls. The two examples that we are going to study in AWS are software-based.

5877
08:40:12,814 --> 08:40:22,896
The infrastructure security part. How do companies classify segments or trusted, untrusted, or highly critical, low-critical public

5878
08:40:23,246 --> 08:40:24,804
zones in their infrastructure?

5879
08:40:25,056 --> 08:40:29,456
Here I'm not talking about a startup that maybe have one or two servers.

5880
08:40:29,536 --> 08:40:39,140
I'm talking about a corporate, a hospital, a university, a government or a ministry in a government that they have a sizable infrastructure

5881
08:40:39,776 --> 08:40:44,068
and they want to protect it according to best practices and according to standards.

5882
08:40:44,590 --> 08:40:50,820
What they do is they start to classify zones in their infrastructure from a security perspective.

5883
08:40:51,696 --> 08:40:53,284
So the zoning architectures.

5884
08:40:54,158 --> 08:40:59,652
Firewalls, as we have seen, can divide the network into trusted and untrusted segments.

5885
08:41:00,016 --> 08:41:04,228
The security architecture of the network infrastructure consists of zones.

5886
08:41:04,416 --> 08:41:07,492
So there are security architects, and this is a job.

5887
08:41:07,936 --> 08:41:18,116
There are infrastructure security architects that will be defining according to compliance needs and standards and the business and so on.

5888
08:41:18,606 --> 08:41:27,428
They will be defining a security architecture that will classify zones and they will detail what should be in each one of these zones.

5889
08:41:29,422 --> 08:41:34,656
So the zoning architecture is designed based on the criticality and the security risk of each component.

5890
08:41:34,862 --> 08:41:37,380
So let's have this example that we have here.

5891
08:41:37,776 --> 08:41:41,060
So I have a firewall. Now we are not showing the routers,

5892
08:41:42,254 --> 08:41:50,256
we are showing the firewalls primarily, and this is an architecture, and that's why it's not a physical drawing.

5893
08:41:50,510 --> 08:42:01,316
So you need to understand, moving into IT, that there are physical diagrams where you have to put the devices and you have to put the ports and the types of the cables,

5894
08:42:01,902 --> 08:42:03,588
speeds, and all that stuff.

5895
08:42:04,336 --> 08:42:13,572
And there are architecture conceptual drawings that explain the concept, and that not necessarily is going to include

5896
08:42:14,096 --> 08:42:15,844
all the devices by name.

5897
08:42:16,398 --> 08:42:17,284
Alright, or type.

5898
08:42:17,806 --> 08:42:22,180
So here we have the internet, and this is a zero trust area.

5899
08:42:22,542 --> 08:42:23,616
We don't trust the internet.

5900
08:42:23,856 --> 08:42:29,348
That's why we have, let's say, firewall one, or we can call it the DMZ firewall.

5901
08:42:29,776 --> 08:42:39,296
That is a firewall that will filter traffic coming in or going out to the internet before it hits the demilitarized zone.

5902
08:42:39,438 --> 08:42:42,340
We call the demilitarized zone, and that's a low trust.

5903
08:42:42,990 --> 08:42:49,092
So it is trusted at a low level because it is only one firewall away from the internet, which is zero trust.

5904
08:42:49,776 --> 08:42:53,764
This is a data center security architecture, by the way.

5905
08:42:54,670 --> 08:42:57,668
So, external DMZ, what would you place? Why do you build it?

5906
08:42:57,936 --> 08:43:01,776
Which workloads are you going to host in an external DMZ?

5907
08:43:02,030 --> 08:43:04,736
So, examples are voice over IP servers.

5908
08:43:04,896 --> 08:43:10,116
If you are using voice over IP, telephony over IP, basically, telephony over the network.

5909
08:43:10,766 --> 08:43:17,696
If you are going to have a file transfer protocol server where people can get in and download files from the outside, from the internet;

5910
08:43:18,222 --> 08:43:21,348
if you have web DNS, VPN,

5911
08:43:22,126 --> 08:43:29,124
or extranet servers that are exposed to the outside, any workloads that should be accessed directly from the internet

5912
08:43:30,016 --> 08:43:36,964
and they are not a high risk or a highly critical workload, then they can be placed in the external DMZ.

5913
08:43:37,216 --> 08:43:37,508
Okay.

5914
08:43:38,016 --> 08:43:50,096
So let's say I have the servers and all that. If these servers they need to communicate, for example, with a database inside, do they just communicate through the network or do we have to have again

5915
08:43:50,256 --> 08:43:55,396
checkpoints, firewalls that are going to process requests before they get in?

5916
08:43:56,046 --> 08:44:08,420
And the answer is if the ones inside are classified as trusted, that means I cannot connect a low trust to a high trust directly through the network, through a switch

5917
08:44:08,656 --> 08:44:09,988
or a router directly.

5918
08:44:10,256 --> 08:44:16,420
I have to put another layer of security or a checkpoint that will do more checks.

5919
08:44:16,816 --> 08:44:26,656
Because if someone from the internet somehow hacks one of the servers here, before they can get in or roam around the infrastructure,

5920
08:44:26,816 --> 08:44:28,420
I have to do another checkpoint.

5921
08:44:28,846 --> 08:44:37,828
Police officers that they will stop them and look at the ID, try to find out what they're trying to go to and why and who are they before they are lit in.

5922
08:44:38,190 --> 08:44:40,256
I'm talking about the traffic they are sending, of

5923
08:44:40,256 --> 08:44:47,204
course. Okay, so what would I have in a high trust zone? You will have applications and maybe end user systems.

5924
08:44:48,878 --> 08:44:52,256
All right, then where are the databases? These are the applications.

5925
08:44:52,416 --> 08:44:53,220
Databases,

5926
08:44:53,934 --> 08:45:01,412
mission critical applications, and IT support critical services or resources, they are going to be in a highest trust.

5927
08:45:01,966 --> 08:45:08,260
Look at the color coding, by the way. The colors usually incorporates and in their zoning architecture they have meanings.

5928
08:45:08,910 --> 08:45:17,316
Here you have the green that is the highest trusted, and the red is the most dangerous one, and that's why it is

5929
08:45:17,616 --> 08:45:20,656
no trust or zero trust, and the color red signifies that.

5930
08:45:20,816 --> 08:45:26,336
If you look from afar, you will be able to tell which zone and what kind of security is that.

5931
08:45:26,446 --> 08:45:32,452
And of course, they document in their documentation, they document the color legend and what that means as

5932
08:45:32,576 --> 08:45:39,876
well. Alright, so if I move from the purple, if it is high trust and I would like to communicate application here, would like to communicate with the database here.

5933
08:45:40,142 --> 08:45:42,852
So it has to go through a layer of firewalls.

5934
08:45:43,694 --> 08:45:49,060
Now, what about the corporate users who are coming through wide area network? They are not coming through the internet.

5935
08:45:50,254 --> 08:45:56,260
Should I fully trust them? And the answer is no. If they are coming from wide area network, we'll have them as medium trust.

5936
08:45:56,558 --> 08:46:01,536
So they are higher trust than the blue, but they are lower trust than the purple and green.

5937
08:46:01,616 --> 08:46:14,096
And that's why we have firewalls behind the WAN routers, the wide area network routers, because they need to protect traffic or filter traffic before it gets into the operational zones.

5938
08:46:14,222 --> 08:46:18,756
And from there, maybe they will try to get into the highest trust zone.

5939
08:46:19,296 --> 08:46:29,296
So this is not something you need to worry about now because that is advanced when we talk about these architectures, and you need to get into network security deeply,

5940
08:46:29,456 --> 08:46:38,336
understand firewalls and how they get configured and their features and so on, before you will be at the architecture level deciding on zoning designs.

5941
08:46:38,606 --> 08:46:45,156
Usually you will enter from the junior to mid to senior engineering before you move into the architecture part.

5942
08:46:48,750 --> 08:47:01,152
All right let's now go into AWS again and introduce one type of virtual firewall, a layer 4 firewall, so it only understands the transport layer, the ports, and the IP addresses,

5943
08:47:01,312 --> 08:47:06,340
cannot get into the application presentation or session or encryption details.

5944
08:47:07,072 --> 08:47:09,552
And that is the security groups.

5945
08:47:09,774 --> 08:47:18,592
This is available also in Microsoft Azure and in Google, and it is a type of a firewall that will protect at the virtual machine level.

5946
08:47:18,832 --> 08:47:21,796
So let's go ahead and discuss that in more details.

5947
08:47:22,958 --> 08:47:29,552
So the security group, what is it? It's a virtual firewall that applies at the instance's network interface.

5948
08:47:29,838 --> 08:47:35,076
So each virtual machine in AWS, as we mentioned earlier, it's called an EC2 instance.

5949
08:47:35,502 --> 08:47:39,632
Elastic Compute Cloud is what EC2 is or abbreviation for.

5950
08:47:40,078 --> 08:47:43,748
And then instance means it's one virtual machine, basically.

5951
08:47:44,238 --> 08:47:51,044
Each virtual machine, as we know from the virtualization concept, it's going to have virtual resources allocated.

5952
08:47:51,232 --> 08:47:53,860
One of them is a virtual network interface.

5953
08:47:55,054 --> 08:48:03,972
So if we look at this symbol as the virtual interface, it's called the ENI in the AWS world, elastic network interface.

5954
08:48:04,654 --> 08:48:07,632
What does it signify? Why did they call it this way? I don't care.

5955
08:48:07,822 --> 08:48:17,316
All we care about is to use the right terms that AWS has used or has decided to use for the VNEC or the virtual network interface.

5956
08:48:17,902 --> 08:48:24,420
So if you heard the term ENI or VNEC, it's exactly the same thing. Virtual network interface card.

5957
08:48:25,262 --> 08:48:28,260
So it's a virtual firewall that applies at the instance network interface.

5958
08:48:28,912 --> 08:48:34,852
And in the security group, we can only allow permit rules, allow rules.

5959
08:48:35,342 --> 08:48:36,836
So how about the deny ones?

5960
08:48:37,102 --> 08:48:39,780
By default, if it is not there, that means it's denied.

5961
08:48:40,782 --> 08:48:47,012
If we have a security group with allow, allow allow and that's it, that means there is an implicit deny at the end.

5962
08:48:47,502 --> 08:48:49,732
So if anything that is not allowed is denied.

5963
08:48:49,934 --> 08:48:55,396
So if I have a blank rule set in a security group, that means everything is denied.

5964
08:48:57,392 --> 08:49:04,644
If you make a change to a security group, the changes are going to take effect immediately. We're going to present all of that, so don't worry about that.

5965
08:49:06,414 --> 08:49:14,212
In a firewall, there is something called inbound and there is something called outbound or ingress and egress.

5966
08:49:15,406 --> 08:49:21,284
But you need to understand the meaning from the vendor perspective, not up to you.

5967
08:49:21,806 --> 08:49:27,748
From the vendor perspective, what is ingress or inbound and what is egress or outbound. For example,

5968
08:49:28,462 --> 08:49:30,852
in some Cisco routers, if you are talking about

5969
08:49:31,854 --> 08:49:37,348
inbound, it could be what is coming into the router and outbound what is going out of the router.

5970
08:49:38,510 --> 08:49:45,952
But here, when we are talking about inbound, that means traffic that is coming eventually into the instance.

5971
08:49:46,222 --> 08:49:53,860
So if the firewall, if the security group allows it, it will go inside the instance. It is sent to the instance.

5972
08:49:54,606 --> 08:50:00,260
Whereas outbound, that means it's initiated from the instance and if allowed it will go out.

5973
08:50:02,926 --> 08:50:08,356
So inbound or ingress means coming into the firewall, distend to the instance.

5974
08:50:09,134 --> 08:50:14,948
Outbound means that it is leaving the instance through the firewall to the outside.

5975
08:50:15,342 --> 08:50:26,468
And the outside could be another instance that is in the same subnet, or it could be another instance in a different subnet in the VPC or even out to the internet. It could be going to anywhere.

5976
08:50:26,584 --> 08:50:34,884
We don't care where it's going, What we care about is outbound means leaving the instance, or the instance has initiated that traffic.

5977
08:50:37,038 --> 08:50:40,672
Outbound is always at the initiating instance site.

5978
08:50:40,846 --> 08:50:47,940
So outbound is always coming from the instance, even if it was going to another instance in the same subnet.

5979
08:50:48,112 --> 08:50:53,152
So this is outbound, and then it will be inbound if allowed to the other instance.

5980
08:50:53,232 --> 08:51:01,220
So please make sure that you understand the flow that you are trying to allow or deny, where it will be initiated from,

5981
08:51:01,552 --> 08:51:11,428
where it is going to, what are the involved ports for the source and destination, and then you will know where you decide. So for example, for this flow,

5982
08:51:14,254 --> 08:51:20,004
assuming that it started from here, let's say this instance is doing a ping or SSH into this instance.

5983
08:51:20,942 --> 08:51:28,964
So it is initiated from here, which means it has to be allowed, if you want to allow it, through the security group

5984
08:51:29,152 --> 08:51:33,796
in order to pass through the security group and continue its trip.

5985
08:51:35,054 --> 08:51:41,828
So as it reaches here, the same flow, the exact same flow, here it is inbound, not outbound.

5986
08:51:42,126 --> 08:51:47,396
So in the inbound rules here, you need to allow it if you want to let it go into the instance.

5987
08:51:48,430 --> 08:51:55,780
If you did not understand that, then definitely you're going to configure that wrong, or maybe you'll do one side and forget about the other side,

5988
08:51:56,352 --> 08:51:59,872
and then the traffic will not flow and you will have problems.

5989
08:51:59,952 --> 08:52:10,912
And we got a lot of questions like that based on lack of understanding, despite the fact we stress time and time again on the importance of understanding the ports,

5990
08:52:11,072 --> 08:52:20,036
understanding the flow, what is the source IP? what's the source port? what's the destination IP? what's the destination port? and now we'll add to that.

5991
08:52:20,272 --> 08:52:28,324
what are the involved security groups or firewalls in the path that need to allow this to get through?

5992
08:52:30,030 --> 08:52:38,372
So here we have to allow it on the outbound in the blue security group, and we have to allow it on the inbound in the receiving security group.

5993
08:52:39,286 --> 08:52:41,232
All right, so let's say this was a ping.

5994
08:52:41,454 --> 08:52:46,116
Ping IP address allowed in the outbound, allowed in the inbound, everything is cool.

5995
08:52:47,214 --> 08:52:53,472
In security groups, they are called to be stateful. They have a memory and they remember the state.

5996
08:52:53,632 --> 08:52:56,548
They remember what was allowed in one direction.

5997
08:52:57,454 --> 08:53:03,972
So the instance will receive the ping and now it's time to respond and say, I am alive. Yes, I'm okay. I got your message.

5998
08:53:04,302 --> 08:53:07,552
As a special case, because it is stateful.

5999
08:53:08,046 --> 08:53:14,596
If the traffic was allowed in one direction, the response for that traffic when it arrives, it will be allowed.

6000
08:53:14,894 --> 08:53:19,172
This is as if you are driving your car into a gated community or a gated compound.

6001
08:53:19,982 --> 08:53:22,752
So when you are coming in, they will ask you where are you going?

6002
08:53:22,926 --> 08:53:25,712
Maybe they will take your phone number and name, which unit you are going to.

6003
08:53:25,792 --> 08:53:33,508
Maybe they will call a landlord or the owner of that unit or the resident in that unit and they will make sure that you are allowed to get in.

6004
08:53:34,350 --> 08:53:35,172
So you get in.

6005
08:53:35,552 --> 08:53:42,112
When you are leaving, the barrier or the gate will open automatically as you approach, and they will not ask you for anything else.

6006
08:53:42,192 --> 08:53:46,020
Or maybe they have cameras that will record that you have left, and that's it.

6007
08:53:47,086 --> 08:53:48,420
This is stateful.

6008
08:53:48,750 --> 08:53:51,844
So that's exactly the same analogy for security groups.

6009
08:53:52,272 --> 08:53:56,356
Is this true for any virtual firewalls or any cloud firewalls?

6010
08:53:56,992 --> 08:54:04,388
Go and check the documentation of every vendor you are dealing with to find out if it is stateless or stateful.

6011
08:54:05,872 --> 08:54:08,100
What if it was not stateful?

6012
08:54:08,512 --> 08:54:15,232
That means you must configure the response rules to be allowed here on the outbound.

6013
08:54:15,406 --> 08:54:20,516
And when it arrives here, then you have to allow the response inbound on this firewall.

6014
08:54:21,286 --> 08:54:28,832
All right, So this is extremely, extremely important to know which firewall you are dealing with. Is it stateful or stateless?

6015
08:54:29,006 --> 08:54:34,272
And should I allow rules in both directions for the forward traffic and the return or the response?

6016
08:54:34,446 --> 08:54:40,164
Or is it enough to do it in one direction and then response is going to work without any problems?

6017
08:54:41,550 --> 08:54:56,164
Let's look at inbound rules and how the format in the security group looks like. So, what do we configure if we wanted to edit or create a security group and define the parameters

6018
08:54:56,592 --> 08:54:58,468
and whether that will be allowed or not?

6019
08:54:58,990 --> 08:55:00,112
So here's an example.

6020
08:55:00,352 --> 08:55:02,532
We need to allow this inbound traffic.

6021
08:55:02,894 --> 08:55:12,644
Let's assume that this is coming from the internet. A user is sitting here and needs to connect to an instance that is in a public subnet connected to the internet.

6022
08:55:14,406 --> 08:55:15,808
All right, what do you need?

6023
08:55:16,878 --> 08:55:22,148
Any instance, any virtual machine created in AWS must have a security group. That's not an option.

6024
08:55:22,368 --> 08:55:26,340
There is no way you can start an instance without a security

6025
08:55:26,368 --> 08:55:37,444
group. And even when you are creating the instance, and we have seen that before and we'll see it again, you are told, do you want to use an existing security group or do you want to create a new one?

6026
08:55:37,998 --> 08:55:41,860
So for the inbound rules, the destination is the instance itself.

6027
08:55:43,406 --> 08:55:48,036
So when you are creating the inbound rules, so this is an example of an inbound rule,

6028
08:55:49,166 --> 08:55:58,724
you can decide on the IP version. It could be IPv4 or IPv6. That means the security group can filter based on IPv4 addresses or IPv6 addresses.

6029
08:55:59,182 --> 08:56:01,636
Then what is the type of the traffic?

6030
08:56:02,062 --> 08:56:08,324
Here we can say HTTP, SSH, all traffic, all TCP. There is a lot of choices. we are going to look at that.

6031
08:56:09,006 --> 08:56:11,460
Then the protocol, is it TCP or UDP?

6032
08:56:12,238 --> 08:56:22,404
Then the port range. Why do they call it a range? Because it could be a single port or it could be a range of ports. If you want, for example, to allow all the ephemeral, all the dynamic port range.

6033
08:56:23,054 --> 08:56:25,828
And then we have the source, and that's it.

6034
08:56:26,510 --> 08:56:27,684
So where is the destination?

6035
08:56:29,230 --> 08:56:31,140
The statement we are trying to make here is

6036
08:56:31,982 --> 08:56:38,820
when the traffic hits the virtual firewall of a specific instance in the inbound direction,

6037
08:56:39,822 --> 08:56:42,608
it is obvious that this is headed to this instance.

6038
08:56:42,798 --> 08:56:45,828
So why should I ask about the destination? I know the destination IP address.

6039
08:56:46,574 --> 08:56:49,316
I know you are headed to the instance I'm trying to protect.

6040
08:56:49,614 --> 08:56:54,628
So the destination is already known. But what we care about is where it is coming from.

6041
08:56:55,214 --> 08:56:59,568
Who are you? Are you allowed to come in? Are you allowed to send traffic in the inbound?

6042
08:56:59,790 --> 08:57:03,728
And we're not talking about only your IP address, but also

6043
08:57:04,528 --> 08:57:07,044
which port on the instance.

6044
08:57:07,438 --> 08:57:14,756
So the port range here is destination port range. We don't ask about the IP, but we ask about the port. Why?

6045
08:57:15,022 --> 08:57:23,780
Because I may have here web, I may have here SSH, I may have here FTP, I may have multiple applications on the server or on the virtual machine.

6046
08:57:24,238 --> 08:57:30,948
So I need to make sure that I know whether you on this IP address you are allowed to communicate with the web.

6047
08:57:31,214 --> 08:57:37,508
Maybe you're only allowed to come into SSH as an administrator, but not to use FTP or the web.

6048
08:57:38,254 --> 08:57:47,268
So it gives you that granularity that you can define based on the source, where it is coming from. The source. So this is the source.

6049
08:57:49,358 --> 08:57:51,488
But the port is on the server.

6050
08:57:51,568 --> 08:57:54,948
So it is very, very important to understand that.

6051
08:57:56,046 --> 08:58:03,808
A lot of the simple and obvious mistakes happen because of this confusion, and people don't know what is the source, where is the source, where is the destination.

6052
08:58:04,014 --> 08:58:05,732
You don't know, you can't figure it out.

6053
08:58:05,968 --> 08:58:09,328
Bring a piece of paper, draw where is the client, what is the flow?

6054
08:58:09,488 --> 08:58:16,996
Somebody is trying to come through TCP and get into the HTTP port on the server, trying to get to a web server.

6055
08:58:17,998 --> 08:58:24,676
So the flow is clear. Is it from within AWS in my VPC? Is it from outside VPC or is it coming from the internet?

6056
08:58:25,358 --> 08:58:26,688
Alright, so it's coming from the internet.

6057
08:58:27,088 --> 08:58:34,276
What are the allowed sources on the internet that can communicate with this virtual machine on the HTTP port?

6058
08:58:35,726 --> 08:58:45,168
And based on that, you will find out. So here, for example, there is only one source that is allowed 98.12.37.32 slash 32.

6059
08:58:45,390 --> 08:58:48,128
So that's a specific IP address, not a range.

6060
08:58:51,086 --> 08:58:53,860
So it has to match everything here as is.

6061
08:58:54,286 --> 08:58:58,116
So this IP address is allowed as a source

6062
08:58:58,926 --> 08:59:02,116
to communicate with the HTTP port on the instance.

6063
08:59:04,430 --> 08:59:05,520
We have a second rule.

6064
08:59:05,680 --> 08:59:07,652
The second rule is about SSH.

6065
08:59:08,654 --> 08:59:12,068
And SSH runs on TCP and on port 22.

6066
08:59:12,782 --> 08:59:15,200
So port 22 is here.

6067
08:59:15,790 --> 08:59:19,204
So we are talking about port 22. Earlier we were talking about port 80.

6068
08:59:19,440 --> 08:59:22,788
Again, that is a port on the instance.

6069
08:59:23,374 --> 08:59:25,220
So this is a destination port.

6070
08:59:25,998 --> 08:59:31,620
The source is 95.12.65.91 slash 32 again.

6071
08:59:32,270 --> 08:59:34,596
So slash 32 means only this IP address.

6072
08:59:35,534 --> 08:59:37,604
So there is another IP address

6073
08:59:38,254 --> 08:59:42,148
that is allowed to come in and to do SSH into the instance.

6074
08:59:42,876 --> 08:59:43,176
All right.

6075
08:59:43,840 --> 08:59:49,120
What if we get an SSH request but coming from a different IP?

6076
08:59:49,280 --> 08:59:54,244
For example, what if this IP address tries to send an SSH into the instance?

6077
08:59:55,054 --> 08:59:55,812
Would it be allowed?

6078
08:59:56,782 --> 08:59:59,876
Security group, inbound, check the rules.

6079
09:00:01,454 --> 09:00:03,972
Cross, cross, we don't have any other rules.

6080
09:00:04,160 --> 09:00:04,484
Deny.

6081
09:00:05,326 --> 09:00:06,148
It will not be allowed.

6082
09:00:06,702 --> 09:00:10,596
What if the second IP address tries to send in an HTTP?

6083
09:00:11,040 --> 09:00:11,524
Again.

6084
09:00:12,206 --> 09:00:13,860
Do I have an HTTP rule? Yes.

6085
09:00:14,254 --> 09:00:15,108
What's the source?

6086
09:00:15,854 --> 09:00:16,964
98.12.

6087
09:00:17,120 --> 09:00:20,548
This is 95.12. No, not allowed.

6088
09:00:21,198 --> 09:00:21,572
Cross.

6089
09:00:22,126 --> 09:00:23,332
So that will be an implicit deny.

6090
09:00:23,600 --> 09:00:28,324
So basically, here there is a deny anything rule that we don't see.

6091
09:00:28,942 --> 09:00:31,396
However, it's an implicit deny, deny all.

6092
09:00:31,982 --> 09:00:34,560
And as you see at the end, there is no allow or deny.

6093
09:00:34,640 --> 09:00:36,548
It's only allow rules in the security group.

6094
09:00:36,960 --> 09:00:45,220
Based on that, the source in the inbound rules is the client, the one who initiated the traffic, and the port is on the instance.

6095
09:00:45,902 --> 09:00:53,732
By the way, if the source was another instance within the VPC, so the source IP will be all also this one.

6096
09:00:54,030 --> 09:01:02,564
So if an instance within the VPC was trying to talk to another instance within the same subnet or any different subnet, the source is the initiator of the traffic,

6097
09:01:02,862 --> 09:01:05,188
the destination is where it is headed.

6098
09:01:06,798 --> 09:01:11,620
Now let's look at the outbound rules, and there will be a difference here, a key difference.

6099
09:01:12,782 --> 09:01:16,772
So let's look at the outbound rule and what information you need to fill in.

6100
09:01:17,390 --> 09:01:20,132
So IP version, same type of traffic,

6101
09:01:20,814 --> 09:01:28,196
same field, same choice, pull-down menu, ICMP, HTTP, SSH, HTTPS, all traffic, all TCP, and so on.

