09:47:32,110 --> 09:47:38,080
So the Knuckle or the Network Access Control List is again a layer for firewall.

6687
09:47:38,160 --> 09:47:41,092
It doesn't understand the upper layers, the application, and so on.

6688
09:47:41,710 --> 09:47:44,196
And it applies at the subnet level.

6689
09:47:44,560 --> 09:47:53,920
Before we proceed and we continue, please note the network ACLs part is an optional part for those who are not getting into cloud engineering,

6690
09:47:54,160 --> 09:47:57,988
who are not getting into network engineering and network security.

6691
09:47:58,446 --> 09:48:05,220
So that is optional. You have already knew what a firewall is and what it can do by learning the security groups.

6692
09:48:05,678 --> 09:48:15,012
So if you are not planning to specialize in one of these three specialties, I think DevOps and MLOps to an extent are also included, then you can skip this part.

6693
09:48:15,310 --> 09:48:24,240
But if you are going to learn the AWS cloud and you're going to build infrastructures in the AWS cloud, no matter what your role is, even if you are a developer,

6694
09:48:24,560 --> 09:48:29,988
then network access control lists are very important because this is part of the security.

6695
09:48:30,318 --> 09:48:39,556
And depending on what you are developing, it can be a mandate, not an optional thing to configure and lock down your network ACLs.

6696
09:48:40,080 --> 09:48:40,708
Alright,

6697
09:48:41,742 --> 09:48:46,788
the network ACL is a virtual firewall that functions at the subnet level.

6698
09:48:47,662 --> 09:48:55,360
Remember, we discussed the security groups, and we said that security groups apply at the instance level, at the virtual machine level.

6699
09:48:55,680 --> 09:48:58,640
Now we are talking about the subnet level.

6700
09:48:58,720 --> 09:49:03,396
So whatever is going to be allowed through the firewall, in or out,

6701
09:49:04,046 --> 09:49:05,540
that will be at the subnet level.

6702
09:49:06,094 --> 09:49:13,200
Whatever is going to be blocked from getting in or going out, it will apply to all the virtual machines in that subnet.

6703
09:49:13,390 --> 09:49:15,044
So this is very important to note.

6704
09:49:15,360 --> 09:49:20,516
The scope of the ACL is the subnet with everything inside that subnet.

6705
09:49:22,702 --> 09:49:28,324
So the network ACL decides what goes into the subnet and what comes out of the subnet.

6706
09:49:29,902 --> 09:49:35,760
The network access control list rules are numbered, unlike security groups, where we just created rules.

6707
09:49:35,920 --> 09:49:44,740
These ones have numbers, and the inspection, when there is some traffic and we need to decide, the network ACL needs to decide whether to allow it or deny it.

6708
09:49:45,120 --> 09:49:56,484
It starts the investigation at the lowest numbered rule, and then the next until a match is found. Once a match is found, it stops the search and then a decision is made.

6709
09:49:58,126 --> 09:50:00,964
And we can configure allow and deny rules.

6710
09:50:01,390 --> 09:50:02,564
Here is an example.

6711
09:50:03,214 --> 09:50:09,892
I have rule 50, rule number 50, rule number 90, rule number 100, and then an asterisk.

6712
09:50:10,446 --> 09:50:16,260
This one is an explicit deny. If no match is found, then there is a deny at the end.

6713
09:50:18,222 --> 09:50:26,628
Parameters or variables that we configure, the type of traffic, SSH, HTTP, all ICMP, all TCP, all UDP, and so on.

6714
09:50:26,894 --> 09:50:34,596
The protocol, what is the port or port range, and what is the source? So this one, for example, is for inbound rules.

6715
09:50:35,406 --> 09:50:37,440
If it was outbound, it will be destination.

6716
09:50:37,582 --> 09:50:39,396
And then we have allow or deny.

6717
09:50:39,520 --> 09:50:42,180
So what is the action if there is a match?

6718
09:50:43,630 --> 09:50:46,788
The security group functions at the virtual machine level.

6719
09:50:47,758 --> 09:50:51,588
And we are saying that the network ACL functions at the subnet level.

6720
09:50:52,078 --> 09:50:53,680
Where does it take effect?

6721
09:50:53,920 --> 09:50:56,720
And the answer is it's at the implied router.

6722
09:50:56,960 --> 09:51:02,180
Remember, we said each subnet is automatically connected to the implied router.

6723
09:51:02,734 --> 09:51:08,068
So the best location to oversee the entire subnet is not to be inside the subnet.

6724
09:51:08,400 --> 09:51:12,612
It is configured at the implied router and it functions at the implied router.

6725
09:51:15,438 --> 09:51:27,972
The impact of the network ACL is applied or applicable to all instances in the respective subnet. So, for example, if on this network ACL we said there is no

6726
09:51:28,846 --> 09:51:32,516
permission for any HTTP to go into the subnet.

6727
09:51:33,550 --> 09:51:35,044
But here I have a web server.

6728
09:51:35,214 --> 09:51:39,940
So this was a let's say a database server, but this was a web server, a website.

6729
09:51:41,902 --> 09:51:45,936
And the website is listening on port 80, which is by default HTTP.

6730
09:51:46,096 --> 09:51:50,564
If you deny HTTP, it will apply to all the instances.

6731
09:51:50,958 --> 09:51:55,748
For this one, it doesn't matter. It's a database and it functions at a different port, not HTTP 80.

6732
09:51:56,398 --> 09:52:07,332
But for this one, what you have just done by denying HTTP to go into the subnet, you have denied any traffic from reaching this web server. That's why this will be broken.

6733
09:52:08,336 --> 09:52:18,820
Having a network ACL configured, you need to make sure that at the network ACL you apply the generic traffic that should be allowed or denied across the subnet.

6734
09:52:19,662 --> 09:52:25,348
Then at the security group level, you can confine, you can refine the allow

6735
09:52:26,416 --> 09:52:33,476
statements to what this virtual machine should receive, and then the rest will be implicitly denied.

6736
09:52:35,950 --> 09:52:37,220
Directions of traffic.

6737
09:52:37,870 --> 09:52:46,532
If the traffic is going into the subnet, so coming from the router and it should pass through the firewall to go into the subnet,

6738
09:52:46,976 --> 09:52:48,196
this is inbound.

6739
09:52:48,656 --> 09:52:49,956
Ingress to the subnet.

6740
09:52:50,862 --> 09:52:59,044
If the traffic is coming out and it should pass through the firewall to go to the router and then somewhere else, that is outbound from the subnet.

6741
09:52:59,534 --> 09:53:04,164
So inbound and outbound are from the perspective of the subnet.

6742
09:53:06,414 --> 09:53:08,256
In security groups, we said they were stateful.

6743
09:53:08,416 --> 09:53:17,828
Stateful means if one if traffic is allowed in one direction, its response will make it through in the opposite direction even if there are no rules to allow it.

6744
09:53:19,246 --> 09:53:23,588
In ACLs, it's completely different. It's stateless.

6745
09:53:24,302 --> 09:53:29,892
So you want to SSH into a server, then you have to allow through the ACL SSH

6746
09:53:30,606 --> 09:53:38,372
in the inbound to the subnet where the server is, you have to allow the response from the virtual machine or the instance

6747
09:53:38,736 --> 09:53:40,740
going out to the user again.

6748
09:53:41,230 --> 09:53:45,060
So you have to allow both directions, otherwise, it will not function.

6749
09:53:46,830 --> 09:53:54,308
So if we want to allow traffic on the inbound, let's say we're going to allow all traffic in the inbound, then what we need to do is we are going to have

6750
09:53:54,990 --> 09:53:59,296
a rule numbered, all traffic from all sources allow.

6751
09:53:59,470 --> 09:54:03,236
If you don't do this, it's going to hit the explicit deny and it will be denied.

6752
09:54:04,654 --> 09:54:12,324
And in the outbound direction, you have to have a rule that will allow traffic as well. Here it's allowing all traffic to all destinations.

6753
09:54:12,656 --> 09:54:14,976
If you don't allow it, it will hit the explicit rule.

6754
09:54:15,056 --> 09:54:17,508
Do you have to do allow all, allow all? Of course not.

6755
09:54:17,616 --> 09:54:19,844
You have to lock it down to what is permissible.

6756
09:54:20,398 --> 09:54:30,500
This is a wide open network ACL on the inbound and outbound direction, and this is not a good security practice, although it is the default in AWS.

6757
09:54:32,270 --> 09:54:36,676
Why do we need the network ACLs if we already have security groups?

6758
09:54:38,734 --> 09:54:42,336
We add an extra layer of protection at the subnet

6759
09:54:42,496 --> 09:54:49,540
level. So you have the security group at the virtual machine level and you have a second layer of defense at the subnet level.

6760
09:54:51,758 --> 09:55:04,324
Also, if you would like to deny specific traffic, let's say we figured out after investigation, that there is a specific range of IP addresses coming from a specific country.

6761
09:55:04,496 --> 09:55:11,684
and that is malicious. that always tries to hack into our website or our network or our VPC presence.

6762
09:55:12,750 --> 09:55:16,772
We would like to deny that traffic completely and allow the rest of the internet.

6763
09:55:17,550 --> 09:55:26,016
So in that case, then since we can do deny and allow, I can have a rule in the inbound to deny the range for any port

6764
09:55:26,256 --> 09:55:27,140
for anything.

6765
09:55:27,822 --> 09:55:38,852
And then afterwards, let's say the first one is rule number 100, the second will be rule 110 or 200, and it will allow traffic from anywhere else.

6766
09:55:40,430 --> 09:55:43,588
Also, if you want to block IP ranges at the subnet level,

6767
09:55:44,816 --> 09:55:51,204
and you are working on a compliance heavy environment where they need multiple layers of protection or firewalls.

