10:44:37,486 --> 10:44:42,144
And using these metrics like CPU utilization, I can find out if everything is okay or not.

7382
10:44:42,318 --> 10:44:51,332
Memory utilization, disk space, network in, network out, all of these are metrics that will tell me by looking at them

7383
10:44:51,744 --> 10:44:54,180
how is the system doing from that perspective.

7384
10:44:54,830 --> 10:44:57,316
Then alerting, notification basically.

7385
10:44:57,518 --> 10:45:03,972
So getting notified when some pre-identified events happen or thresholds are crossed is a good thing.

7386
10:45:04,238 --> 10:45:05,316
That's what alerting is.

7387
10:45:06,222 --> 10:45:09,504
So it could be performance related, could be health related or security related.

7388
10:45:09,584 --> 10:45:18,544
For example, if someone is trying to SSH into an instance and it doesn't pass, it doesn't succeed, and it fails repeatedly,

7389
10:45:18,784 --> 10:45:21,380
I need to know someone is trying to hack my systems.

7390
10:45:21,504 --> 10:45:22,052
For example,

7391
10:45:23,054 --> 10:45:31,904
if the CPU utilization of one of the critical server reaches 80% or 90 %, I need to be notified even if it is at 3 o'clock in the morning,

7392
10:45:32,064 --> 10:45:38,944
because that is a critical thing that will affect the response time, that will affect the performance of the server and application,

7393
10:45:39,086 --> 10:45:42,596
and that will cause a lot of issues when users will start to complain.

7394
10:45:44,654 --> 10:45:50,020
One of the examples where you can be notified in AWS is when you have a budget alarm.

7395
10:45:50,606 --> 10:46:01,092
So basically, you tell AWS, you know what, if I am getting the consumption is expected to cross or be higher than $3.60

7396
10:46:01,984 --> 10:46:05,732
month, please send me an email or send me a notification.

7397
10:46:05,966 --> 10:46:13,984
And here's an example of a notification that I have received when the cost, the budget amount for the month, I don't want to spend more than six

7398
10:46:14,144 --> 10:46:26,052
dollars, but please let me know when I reach 60% of that. So I was told that it's going to get to $4.84 if you keep the same utilization for the month.

7399
10:46:26,798 --> 10:46:33,636
So this is an example of notification where I was informed based on a configuration I have done about this event.

7400
10:46:34,350 --> 10:46:38,464
If it didn't cross the threshold, I wouldn't have received any emails or anything.

7401
10:46:38,702 --> 10:46:41,744
Similarly for security, similarly for performance.

7402
10:46:42,254 --> 10:46:49,732
Then we have logging. Logging is an archive storage of events or errors related to a system.

7403
10:46:50,766 --> 10:46:59,844
So if you are logging, for example, who had access to your account, who had created EC2 instances in your account, who had deleted EC2 instances in your account,

7404
10:47:00,174 --> 10:47:11,364
if you can find these logs, if a problem happens, then we can go back to the logs and find out who did what and at what time to what service and who deleted and or who created and so on.

7405
10:47:11,824 --> 10:47:17,636
So this is good both for auditing if you are in a business that requires compliance

7406
10:47:18,510 --> 10:47:24,996
and also for troubleshooting if we need to find out the reason for the problem that happened or the outage that happened.

7407
10:47:26,254 --> 10:47:30,052
And in AWS we have a service called Cloud Trail.

7408
10:47:30,624 --> 10:47:39,332
Any logins, any sign-ins, any creation of virtual machines, any deletion of virtual machines, any creation of subnets, VPC, root tables,

7409
10:47:39,694 --> 10:47:42,884
security groups, network ACLs, all are going to be logged here.

7410
10:47:43,184 --> 10:47:51,428
Any action on AWS services in your account is going to be logged here with the time it has happened, who did it, which user,

7411
10:47:51,824 --> 10:48:00,452
and what was the event source, and so on. A lot of details. And if you click one of these links, you will find that out. Let's look at this service.

7412
10:48:01,198 --> 10:48:04,036
So I'm going to go here and type Cloud Trail

7413
10:48:04,846 --> 10:48:10,084
and I'm going to open a new tab. We have so many tabs open now, but let's go into this service.

7414
10:48:10,464 --> 10:48:15,620
And this is enabled by default in your account, in any AWS account, and you don't pay for it.

7415
10:48:17,134 --> 10:48:25,636
So if I look here, if I click here and I look at the event history, and this is of course about my account, and this is the account ID, you'll find here that

7416
10:48:26,734 --> 10:48:30,692
send SSH public key, and here it has the date, who did it,

7417
10:48:31,424 --> 10:48:38,624
and easy to instance and the ID of the instance that was affected with that. And if we click on that, you will find more details.

7418
10:48:38,734 --> 10:48:44,864
So you'll find the timestamp, who did it, the user, what was the event name, event source, event ID.

7419
10:48:46,862 --> 10:48:50,816
You have here the instance ID and the details of the event.

7420
10:48:51,022 --> 10:48:58,976
I don't want to drag you into what this means right now, but this is a lot of information that is very useful when we need

7421
10:48:59,136 --> 10:49:03,108
to troubleshoot or find out what has happened and who did it and so on.

7422
10:49:03,598 --> 10:49:08,416
And those events are kept by default for 90 days free of charge.

7423
10:49:08,622 --> 10:49:16,260
After 90 days, they are purged, and if you want to keep them, you can export them and save them in a storage that you will pay for.

7424
10:49:17,134 --> 10:49:20,644
Also, we have a service called CloudWatch, and this is

7425
10:49:21,536 --> 10:49:26,564
for monitoring in your account and for logging in your account and for alerting as well.

7426
10:49:26,656 --> 10:49:32,976
So it has, as you can see, it has insights, metrics, alarms, dashboards, and services.

7427
10:49:33,070 --> 10:49:36,196
So let's have a look at this service, Cloud Watch.

7428
10:49:37,358 --> 10:49:44,964
For example, if I go into metrics, all metrics, scroll down a little bit, you'll find here EC2, for example, virtual machines.

7429
10:49:45,136 --> 10:49:46,468
And if I click on that,

7430
10:49:47,758 --> 10:49:48,336
I have

7431
10:49:49,390 --> 10:49:51,376
metric data available.

7432
10:49:51,662 --> 10:49:53,860
If I start scrolling, as you can see here.

7433
10:49:54,222 --> 10:49:58,628
For example, this is the CPU utilization average on EC2.

7434
10:49:59,022 --> 10:50:06,372
So you can see that the CPU utilization is low right now, but it has gone up to six uh percent.

7435
10:50:07,534 --> 10:50:14,436
If we keep going, network average, network in average, the traffic going into the instance, the average,

7436
10:50:15,950 --> 10:50:23,536
and we have also the network packet out average, network packet in average, network out average.

7437
10:50:23,694 --> 10:50:27,460
So you have lots of details about what's happening for this instance.

7438
10:50:28,334 --> 10:50:29,776
So that is for the metrics.

7439
10:50:29,936 --> 10:50:33,028
What else can I see from CloudWatch if I go back to the dashboard?

7440
10:50:33,390 --> 10:50:34,564
You can create alarms.

7441
10:50:38,478 --> 10:50:44,896
So I can create alarms, alarms, and the alarm will watch for a single metric. For example, the CPU of the virtual machine.

7442
10:50:44,976 --> 10:50:53,296
So I can monitor the CPU, the average CPU of the virtual machine, and I can instruct it if it goes above 75%, send me a notification, for example.

7443
10:50:53,390 --> 10:50:55,332
That is possible also to do in CloudWatch.

7444
10:50:56,718 --> 10:50:58,404
Then we have CloudWatch logs.

7445
10:50:58,496 --> 10:51:08,096
So I can send the logs from all my applications and the errors from all the applications and the events that happen, and I can save it in a log group into

7446
10:51:08,256 --> 10:51:09,092
CloudWatch.

7447
10:51:09,216 --> 10:51:14,884
So CloudWatch is the core or the heart of the monitoring as far as AWS is concerned.

7448
10:51:15,136 --> 10:51:16,336
So why is it important?

7449
10:51:16,462 --> 10:51:17,700
Let's go back and find out.

7450
10:51:18,062 --> 10:51:20,868
The importance of monitoring, alerting, and logging,

7451
10:51:21,710 --> 10:51:26,340
for security perspective, you need to know if someone is attacking or probing your system.

7452
10:51:26,830 --> 10:51:30,212
If someone is trying to break into your system or scanning your system for attacks,

7453
10:51:31,056 --> 10:51:40,036
you need this monitoring, alerting, and logging for troubleshooting if you want to fix things faster in a proactive manner when something goes wrong

7454
10:51:40,416 --> 10:51:42,596
before the users start to complain.

7455
10:51:44,096 --> 10:51:50,084
For performance, you need to monitor your performance and spot slowness before the users do.

7456
10:51:50,736 --> 10:51:55,396
Because you want to make sure that they are going to have the right response and the right performance.

7457
10:51:55,950 --> 10:52:03,136
For compliance, the logs are very important because we need to keep records of what's happening in the infrastructure for audits and policies.

7458
10:52:03,406 --> 10:52:11,428
So these are many, many reasons why we need to make sure that monitoring is in place, alerting is in place, and logging is also in place.

7459
10:52:13,134 --> 10:52:16,496
Example use cases CPU usage goes over 80%.

7460
10:52:16,896 --> 10:52:17,856
I need to be notified.

7461
10:52:17,936 --> 10:52:23,396
Someone tries to SSH into a virtual machine repeatedly and they don't succeed.

7462
10:52:23,856 --> 10:52:32,036
A part of the code or the application code fails to run. I need to have that error sent into logging or maybe a notification based on that.

7463
10:52:32,654 --> 10:52:35,216
Website response time is going down.

7464
10:52:35,406 --> 10:52:37,828
I need to know about that as well as it happens.

7465
10:52:38,798 --> 10:52:40,976
A web server runs out of disk space.

7466
10:52:41,136 --> 10:52:46,340
I need to find out before that starts to impact the performance or the user experience.

7467
10:52:49,198 --> 10:52:58,116
Example tools Windows test Manager, Mac OS Activity Monitor, AWS Cloud Watch for monitoring, alerting and logging, and AWS Cloud Trail for logging.

7468
10:53:00,878 --> 10:53:06,500
We talked a lot about databases, but we didn't dig deeper into what a database is and why do we need it.

7469
10:53:07,918 --> 10:53:15,424
To understand the importance of databases in applications or in IT, think about a pizza shop.

7470
10:53:15,504 --> 10:53:23,972
So imagine you are running that shop and you need to keep track of the customer orders, who ordered what, and maybe the customer phone number or name.

7471
10:53:25,614 --> 10:53:29,316
Ingredients used, deliveries and payments.

7472
10:53:30,222 --> 10:53:35,652
And if you want all of that written down in a lookbook that is going to be your database.

7473
10:53:36,590 --> 10:53:39,588
And that may run for a small pizza shop.

7474
10:53:40,078 --> 10:53:48,324
But for a popular pizza shop that has so many branches, a franchise, people can order online and deliveries and all that.

7475
10:53:48,558 --> 10:53:55,504
There is no way one person or even a couple of persons or three people will be writing down all of that manually.

7476
10:53:55,664 --> 10:54:01,252
It has to be in the application, it has to be in a persistent storage that will keep this data.

7477
10:54:02,510 --> 10:54:09,540
So if we shift now into IT, the database is an organized collection of data that can be easily stored,

7478
10:54:10,286 --> 10:54:13,664
searched, updated, and retrieved by software.

7479
10:54:13,824 --> 10:54:23,424
So the application can write in the database, can search the data in the database, can update the data in the database, and can retrieve data from the database.

7480
10:54:23,630 --> 10:54:31,332
And even if the application fails or whatever, the data in the database is going to persist. And we usually take copies, we call it backups.

7481
10:54:31,758 --> 10:54:37,508
So in case if the database fails, we can retrieve or restore from the backup into a new database.

7482
10:54:40,302 --> 10:54:44,132
One might ask, okay, but I can do this in Microsoft Excel.

7483
10:54:44,384 --> 10:54:52,996
I can have all the data about the customers and the orders and what pizza they ordered and how many and how much they paid and all that stuff in an Excel sheet?

7484
10:54:53,550 --> 10:54:55,104
And the answer it's okay, fine.

7485
10:54:55,184 --> 10:54:59,172
You can do it in an Excel sheet or in a Word file. but again, scaling.

7486
10:55:00,686 --> 10:55:02,372
how easy it is to search.

7487
10:55:02,624 --> 10:55:10,544
would an application be able to read the Excel file or ask the Excel file for a specific information and get the proper response,

7488
10:55:10,624 --> 10:55:16,484
or does it have to search the Excel file every time it needs to get a part of the information?

7489
10:55:16,910 --> 10:55:25,104
So we need a system that can receive a short question, look through a database engine into the data,

7490
10:55:25,264 --> 10:55:26,884
and then respond with the answer.

7491
10:55:27,182 --> 10:55:30,372
So the system itself, the database itself, is going to take care of that.

7492
10:55:31,758 --> 10:55:42,372
We may think of database as like superpowered spreadsheets and they have the search capability within, so no need for us or for the application to search by itself.

7493
10:55:42,944 --> 10:55:45,956
Why do applications need databases?

7494
10:55:46,446 --> 10:55:49,412
I think we have mentioned that already, but let's summarize it.

7495
10:55:50,126 --> 10:55:56,868
So applications they need to store the user data like usernames, messages, customer orders, and so on.

7496
10:55:57,358 --> 10:56:03,940
So the user interfaces with the application and the application writes into the database and gets a confirmation to that.

7497
10:56:04,782 --> 10:56:10,660
E-commerce sites storing customer orders, customer preferences, customer refunds, and so

7498
10:56:11,438 --> 10:56:14,436
on. Hospitals will need to store the patient records.

7499
10:56:15,024 --> 10:56:18,692
Banks will store banking transactions or financial transactions.

7500
10:56:20,206 --> 10:56:25,024
With databases, it's always called an input and output or write and read operations.

7501
10:56:25,198 --> 10:56:29,504
When we write to the database, the application will write to store data.

7502
10:56:29,678 --> 10:56:37,744
So the user, for example, goes on dolphinet.com, buys one of the bundles, and now this is going to store

7503
10:56:37,984 --> 10:56:40,164
the order information in the database.

7504
10:56:40,974 --> 10:56:44,864
Receive a confirmation and then confirm to the client that everything is okay.

7505
10:56:45,070 --> 10:56:49,572
So your order is processed and here is your access, or this is what you will get.

7506
10:56:50,862 --> 10:56:52,260
So this is for the right operations.

7507
10:56:52,942 --> 10:57:04,944
But also we can do query operations or read operations. For example, I go on Amazon.com and I need to find out my orders for the past three months because there is one item I need to reorder it

7508
10:57:04,944 --> 10:57:11,044
again. So now the application logic will understand what you want as you click on orders.

7509
10:57:11,374 --> 10:57:13,220
Three months, show me the history.

7510
10:57:13,518 --> 10:57:19,300
It will query the database and ask for the history of your orders in the last three months.

7511
10:57:20,110 --> 10:57:26,948
Gets the answer, prepares how the answer would look like in a nice way on the page, and then you will get that in your browser.

7512
10:57:28,782 --> 10:57:31,588
There are types of database, there are not all one type.

7513
10:57:32,494 --> 10:57:40,432
So there are relational databases where the data is sorted out in tables, and examples, database that are relational, Postgres,

7514
10:57:40,752 --> 10:57:42,148
MySQL, and SQL Server.

7515
10:57:43,854 --> 10:57:47,780
NoSQL database, examples, Mongo database and AWS Dynamo DB.

7516
10:57:48,494 --> 10:57:50,192
In memory database, very fast.

7517
10:57:50,478 --> 10:57:52,484
Redis is the best example.

7518
10:57:53,038 --> 10:58:05,092
And Time Series, we have Influx, Amazon TimeStream. So we have many types of database. The most common one is the relational one, but also the NoSQL is gaining popularity.

7519
10:58:05,582 --> 10:58:09,312
An example of a relational database, it's exactly a neat table.

7520
10:58:09,550 --> 10:58:13,124
So for example, here we have the order ID, customer name,

7521
10:58:14,094 --> 10:58:17,892
the pizza, which type, the quantity, and the price paid.

7522
10:58:18,446 --> 10:58:24,912
So this is an example that would be stored in a database, and I can search it and I can find it at the end of the day.

7523
10:58:25,646 --> 10:58:28,420
How can we read and write from a database?

7524
10:58:29,870 --> 10:58:33,872
This is following something called SQL, structured query language.

7525
10:58:34,382 --> 10:58:39,428
So using SQL, you can write and read from database, especially the relational database.

7526
10:58:39,758 --> 10:58:43,684
So allows applications to talk to the database, read or write using simple queries.

7527
10:58:43,872 --> 10:58:44,932
And here are examples.

7528
10:58:45,870 --> 10:58:50,148
So if I have a table with the name order, so a database with the name orders,

7529
10:58:50,926 --> 10:58:52,772
and this is the information in the table,

7530
10:58:53,998 --> 10:59:00,708
then I can do a query to find information from the database, select, and this is SQL command.

7531
10:59:01,472 --> 10:59:02,116
Select

7532
10:59:02,862 --> 10:59:08,164
asterisk from the table orders where customer equals Bob.

7533
10:59:09,166 --> 10:59:10,832
So what is it that I'm finding here?

7534
10:59:11,072 --> 10:59:14,500
Can you find me in the table with the name orders?

7535
10:59:15,342 --> 10:59:23,716
Search all the items and find me the entries or the records where the customer equals the name is Bob.

7536
10:59:25,134 --> 10:59:28,132
So this is the customer field and this is Bob.

7537
10:59:28,398 --> 10:59:31,620
So basically the output for this one is going to be this row.

7538
10:59:32,590 --> 10:59:38,116
So this query will request the table is through all orders in the table, named orders where the customer is Bob.

7539
10:59:40,878 --> 10:59:43,332
Again, for the same table, I can

7540
10:59:44,078 --> 10:59:48,452
do other queries will be around how many pizzas has Bob ordered.

7541
10:59:48,974 --> 10:59:50,660
So if I'm looking for this

7542
10:59:51,630 --> 10:59:52,164
from Bob,

7543
10:59:52,942 --> 10:59:54,752
or what's the total revenue today?

7544
10:59:54,926 --> 10:59:58,116
So I want the collection of the prices paid.

7545
10:59:58,734 --> 11:00:01,220
Or show all orders with pepperoni pizza.

7546
11:00:01,614 --> 11:00:08,832
So these are examples of, if you build the code to do these functions, then you can easily go open the application.

7547
11:00:09,006 --> 11:00:21,952
click "show me all the revenue for today ," or "show me, how many pepperoni pizza were orders ," or "how? many packs of mushroom were used today in the ingredients ," or cheese or whatever.

7548
11:00:22,112 --> 11:00:28,032
So databases, the good thing about them is they can be integrated with applications and they are persistent.

7549
11:00:28,192 --> 11:00:38,628
Persistent means they are going to maintain the data, even if issues happen in the infrastructure, the data will still be there. Even if the application fails, the data will still be there.

7550
11:00:39,086 --> 11:00:50,592
As long as I have the right way or the right process of backing up, of taking copies or snapshots from the database on a regular base, so such that even if the database fails,

7551
11:00:50,752 --> 11:00:58,596
the infrastructure fails, I can take that data, populate it in a new database, and restore the functionality of the applications.

7552
11:00:59,726 --> 11:01:11,872
So databases are extremely important, and this is an integral part of any infrastructure that you are going to work on. Be it for AI, be it for machine learning, be it for cybersecurity or anything.

7553
11:01:12,112 --> 11:01:14,436
Always, always, always you will need a database.

7554
11:01:19,022 --> 11:01:20,708
Let's do a hands-on demo

