12:27:16,672 --> 12:27:19,044
One of them, which is legacy, is waterfall.

8674
12:27:19,232 --> 12:27:19,940
That's traditional.

8675
12:27:21,358 --> 12:27:28,004
So in waterfall, we expect that the development team is going to sit and work for six months or 12 months

8676
12:27:28,686 --> 12:27:36,196
on the software, and then when ready, it will be shifted to testing, and when ready, it will be shifted to operation or release and then operations.

8677
12:27:36,718 --> 12:27:39,076
So this is the old way, sequential methodology.

8678
12:27:39,662 --> 12:27:48,992
So we look at the requirements, we look at the design or design phase, coding or development, building and testing, deployment and operation and maintenance and monitoring,

8679
12:27:49,152 --> 12:27:54,276
and only when this is done, then we can take new requirements and we can work on a different project.

8680
12:27:54,894 --> 12:27:55,876
But of course, this is

8681
12:27:56,686 --> 12:28:01,956
only or or can fit with projects that have well-defined requirements.

8682
12:28:02,352 --> 12:28:04,432
Today, every day we need a new feature.

8683
12:28:04,592 --> 12:28:07,204
We get customer feedback and then we need to build a new feature.

8684
12:28:08,014 --> 12:28:16,196
The fact that we can have all the requirements up front with no changes for six or twelve months is very rare to happen nowadays.

8685
12:28:17,230 --> 12:28:20,964
Releases here are very slow every six or twelve months to have a release.

8686
12:28:21,806 --> 12:28:23,392
And the testing is done manually.

8687
12:28:23,472 --> 12:28:26,372
So this is completely, as you can see, waterfall is

8688
12:28:27,054 --> 12:28:29,284
completely off the goal that we had.

8689
12:28:29,678 --> 12:28:33,188
Manual testing, and it only happens after development is complete.

8690
12:28:33,998 --> 12:28:37,444
And operation teams will deploy the code, and of course, this is Silos.

8691
12:28:37,712 --> 12:28:44,964
We are talking here about operations team at their ease, whenever they have time, they are going to look at the code and look into deploying that.

8692
12:28:46,318 --> 12:28:48,228
Then evolved agile.

8693
12:28:48,592 --> 12:28:51,972
And agile is more of a

8694
12:28:52,718 --> 12:28:59,460
set of principles or methodology, it's not only about project management, that is used in software development.

8695
12:29:00,032 --> 12:29:00,548
Agile

8696
12:29:01,646 --> 12:29:02,852
is an iterative

8697
12:29:03,662 --> 12:29:06,148
and flexible software development methodology.

8698
12:29:06,734 --> 12:29:14,692
Usually in Agile, we agree on a milestone, it could be a feature, it could be a release, and it is divided into sprints.

8699
12:29:15,278 --> 12:29:18,052
Let's say the sprint will be two to four weeks. We are going to work

8700
12:29:18,766 --> 12:29:21,732
on code, and every two to four weeks we are going to test and release.

8701
12:29:22,958 --> 12:29:26,116
So the goal is to deliver working software more frequently.

8702
12:29:26,670 --> 12:29:27,952
The waterfall had two issues.

8703
12:29:28,112 --> 12:29:31,812
The first one is it does not accommodate new requirements during the workflow.

8704
12:29:32,718 --> 12:29:35,236
Second thing, it takes very long for that

8705
12:29:36,046 --> 12:29:44,900
to complete before testing happens, and of course, a long list of errors and bugs and all that will be discovered and integration issues that needs to be fixed, and it takes a long time to be fixed.

8706
12:29:45,472 --> 12:29:48,032
So Agile said, okay, fine, we're going to fix that.

8707
12:29:48,078 --> 12:29:53,872
we're going to iterate through the process. So we're not going to have one long process for development workflow.

8708
12:29:54,032 --> 12:29:58,276
Development is too long, and then testing is too long, and release and deployment is too long.

8709
12:29:58,766 --> 12:30:06,992
We are going to do that frequently, and we can gather new requirements and we can add it into the new sprints, the new milestones, and we can work

8710
12:30:07,152 --> 12:30:10,436
on it, test and everything, and release every two to four weeks.

8711
12:30:12,526 --> 12:30:20,388
So as you can see here, we have planning, we have a plan phase, we have design, coding, building and testing, deploy

8712
12:30:20,592 --> 12:30:22,916
and review, and then it repeats.

8713
12:30:23,152 --> 12:30:30,980
So we can launch or deploy, and then we have again planning, gathering new requirements, designing, and so on.

8714
12:30:31,278 --> 12:30:40,068
So it is a process that repeats at high frequency or close intervals, so we can release at close intervals as well.

8715
12:30:41,454 --> 12:30:50,112
And testing is continuous, but it's semi-manual, so it is very close to what we wanted, but the testing is semi-manual, and also two to four weeks is a bit long.

8716
12:30:50,286 --> 12:30:53,060
So we are still aiming for a better solution.

8717
12:30:54,222 --> 12:31:03,632
And the release manager coordinates development team and operations team to work on the deployment, but of course, it all depends on how available are the operations team

8718
12:31:03,792 --> 12:31:07,780
because Agile focuses more on the development phase and not on the operations phase.

8719
12:31:09,262 --> 12:31:17,220
So the limitations in Agile, before DevOps, of course, it was widely adopted to make software development faster and more responsive, as we explained.

8720
12:31:17,870 --> 12:31:21,888
However, Agile ended at the code handoff to operations.

8721
12:31:21,998 --> 12:31:26,948
So once the code is ready, test it and then release manager coordinates the deployment, that's

8722
12:31:27,790 --> 12:31:30,128
it. So is the operations team agile?

8723
12:31:30,288 --> 12:31:31,396
The answer was no.

8724
12:31:31,758 --> 12:31:36,484
It did not transform operations, it did not include operations in the agile process.

8725
12:31:36,878 --> 12:31:38,820
And that is a problem because now we have

8726
12:31:39,566 --> 12:31:45,796
a team that is collaborating the testing and QA and all that and developers, but then the operation is a different silo.

8727
12:31:47,406 --> 12:31:51,620
An operations team, because they're not agile, they still used slow manual processes.

8728
12:31:52,654 --> 12:32:04,708
And this caused friction, of course, between the development teams and the operations teams. And that is what necessitated finding out a new solution to this, which is DevOps.

8729
12:32:09,326 --> 12:32:10,532
So let's talk about DevOps.

8730
12:32:11,566 --> 12:32:14,500
DevOps increased the automation

8731
12:32:15,406 --> 12:32:25,636
and also married development, QA testing, and operations. Brought all of these and blended them in one bowl, and this is the team responsible for the software delivery.

8732
12:32:27,150 --> 12:32:32,804
So the problem before DevOps that Agile created a fast dev slow ops bottleneck.

8733
12:32:33,678 --> 12:32:34,660
So we needed to fix that.

8734
12:32:35,502 --> 12:32:39,945
the code was ready every two weeks, but ops teams deployed monthly or quarterly

8735
12:32:41,230 --> 12:32:43,204
And developers and ops worked in silos.

8736
12:32:43,488 --> 12:32:45,028
These were the issues that we discussed.

8737
12:32:45,486 --> 12:32:46,288
So what is DevOps?

8738
12:32:46,528 --> 12:32:53,572
DevOps is a set of practices, tools, and a culture mindset that combines software development, development, and IT operation.

8739
12:32:54,382 --> 12:32:57,636
It brings both of them as a collaborative bigger team.

8740
12:32:58,126 --> 12:33:05,008
It's a full software lifecycle investment and it's a team undertaking. It's not one person that will decide I'm going to do DevOps.

8741
12:33:05,248 --> 12:33:08,516
It has to be adopted and managed by the team.

8742
12:33:09,070 --> 12:33:17,476
And DevOps enables better software development and delivery practices because it's going to fix or solve the bottleneck caused by the operations in Agile.

8743
12:33:18,030 --> 12:33:20,932
And it accelerates the last mile of continuous delivery.

8744
12:33:22,830 --> 12:33:31,460
So that means DevOps did not cancel Agile. However, it extended Agile beyond dev into the deployment, infrastructure, operation, monitoring, and maintenance.

8745
12:33:33,070 --> 12:33:40,928
And it brought automation, collaboration, and shared responsibility across the entire software lifecycle or SDLC,

8746
12:33:41,088 --> 12:33:44,420
not only until the coding and testing is done as Agile did.

8747
12:33:45,998 --> 12:33:47,524
Goals and benefits of DevOps:

8748
12:33:48,334 --> 12:33:49,604
faster software delivery,

8749
12:33:50,574 --> 12:33:55,780
and improved collaboration between teams, more reliable releases, so more automation,

8750
12:33:56,942 --> 12:33:59,328
and automated testing, deployment, and monitoring.

8751
12:33:59,488 --> 12:34:05,348
That's the way we can have better quality, better scalability and performance, and

8752
12:34:06,190 --> 12:34:10,688
it will promote the collaboration among the different teams involved in the software delivery

8753
12:34:10,688 --> 12:34:17,220
process. So let's look at the areas or practices that are now considered under DevOps.

8754
12:34:18,446 --> 12:34:20,228
First one is continuous integration.

8755
12:34:20,448 --> 12:34:26,532
Every time the developers are going to make changes to the code, they will commit that code in a repository, GitHub, for example.

8756
12:34:27,248 --> 12:34:31,044
And that is going to automatically trigger the build and test.

8757
12:34:31,630 --> 12:34:35,556
So every time there are changes, build and test is going to be run automatically.

8758
12:34:35,886 --> 12:34:45,796
And then we get into the continuous delivery, which is once build and test are done and feedback is done, iterations have been taken care of and no errors now,

8759
12:34:46,062 --> 12:34:49,348
then software will be ready for release into production.

8760
12:34:50,958 --> 12:34:58,372
Also, infrastructure as code, which is scripting the configurations or the infrastructures that you build, the resources that you need

8761
12:34:59,008 --> 12:35:04,804
in Terraform, for example, scripts or Python scripts, or maybe CloudFormation scripts and so on.

8762
12:35:07,630 --> 12:35:12,048
Then we have automated testing to have less errors and faster testing processes.

8763
12:35:12,238 --> 12:35:21,796
And then configuration management, which is all about centralizing and standardizing how the software and the applications are being configured and set up after they are deployed.

8764
12:35:26,062 --> 12:35:34,724
Now let's look at the first two practices that we have looked at the continuous integration and the continuous delivery, and we'll add to that also the continuous deployment.

8765
12:35:37,390 --> 12:35:42,244
Taking a step back, CICD is all about continuous integration, continuous delivery, and continuous deployment.

8766
12:35:42,894 --> 12:35:51,568
And these are software development practices in DevOps that are aimed at automating software development end-to-end, including the marriage between the

8767
12:35:51,968 --> 12:35:53,444
development and operations.

8768
12:35:54,222 --> 12:36:00,996
This will yield better software, better higher quality, faster releases, and better user experience as well.

8769
12:36:01,582 --> 12:36:02,756
Let's look at CI.

8770
12:36:04,814 --> 12:36:08,448
So CI or continuous integration refers

8771
12:36:09,358 --> 12:36:18,404
to the DevOps practice that is going to trigger automatically a workflow each time developers are going to commit code changes.

8772
12:36:19,214 --> 12:36:26,244
So developers, every time they commit code changes, so they make changes and they upload them or push them into GitHub,

8773
12:36:27,534 --> 12:36:31,492
that is going to trigger the CI tools to build and test.

8774
12:36:32,526 --> 12:36:35,044
And that is going to be the continuous integration.

8775
12:36:35,214 --> 12:36:44,368
So automatically triggering the build and test process and the iterations and feedback until the software is ready for release in a staging

8776
12:36:44,688 --> 12:36:45,408
environment.

8777
12:36:45,728 --> 12:36:47,812
This is what CI is.

8778
12:36:52,110 --> 12:36:57,156
Now let's look at the other part of CI CD, which is continuous delivery and continuous deployment.

8779
12:36:57,408 --> 12:36:58,340
Both are CD.

8780
12:36:59,438 --> 12:37:00,388
So this is CI.

8781
12:37:03,438 --> 12:37:04,708
Continuous delivery means

8782
12:37:05,422 --> 12:37:13,380
then once the release, the software is ready for release, ready to go in production, there will be a manual process, manual approval, manual scheduling,

8783
12:37:13,838 --> 12:37:16,132
and then it will go into production deployment.

8784
12:37:18,510 --> 12:37:19,396
So code changes

8785
12:37:20,174 --> 12:37:26,788
to a staging, testing, or production environment after the code build step, but with a manual approval, with a manual process.

8786
12:37:27,886 --> 12:37:33,648
So this is dependent on the team's plans and decisions for release time. Changes need to be released.

8787
12:37:35,150 --> 12:37:44,228
In continuous deployment, changes that pass all the CI, the build and test steps, are deployed automatically without human intervention.

8788
12:37:45,486 --> 12:37:48,068
And of course, because there is no human intervention,

8789
12:37:49,038 --> 12:37:53,828
this is going to be risky and that's why it's not commonly used. Not in the critical applications, at least.

8790
12:37:54,208 --> 12:37:58,820
What is more common is CI continuous integration and continuous delivery.

8791
12:37:59,168 --> 12:38:05,796
Releasing and making sure that it's available, and once it is reviewed or approved, then it will be deployed.

8792
12:38:17,838 --> 12:38:24,772
And because this is very important and it causes a lot of confusion, I decided that let's go ahead and discuss it in a video.

8793
12:38:26,894 --> 12:38:30,448
So do you have to be a developer or a programmer to work as DevOps engineer?

8794
12:38:30,608 --> 12:38:32,868
The short answer is no, you don't have

8795
12:38:33,008 --> 12:38:42,980
to. But let's explain the two schools of thought and the reason why there is a lot of DevOps engineer jobs that will expect you to be a developer.

8796
12:38:43,310 --> 12:38:53,248
So based on the company, and it differs from company to company and project to project, the two schools of thought are one expects you as a DevOps engineer to be a professional developer.

8797
12:38:53,328 --> 12:38:55,524
So you have been a developer and then you got into DevOps.

8798
12:38:56,526 --> 12:39:01,924
And the other one would say we only need you to have the fundamentals of programming.

8799
12:39:02,446 --> 12:39:10,368
So you have the logic, you know the data types and data structures, and also you can do scripting and you can do file operations,

8800
12:39:10,608 --> 12:39:12,388
like simple stuff using code.

8801
12:39:12,750 --> 12:39:15,300
And of course, Python would be the easiest to learn in that case.

8802
12:39:16,142 --> 12:39:25,284
So the short answer is, you don't have to be a full stack developer or a back-end developer, but you must know the fundamentals of programming.

8803
12:39:26,030 --> 12:39:30,660
you must know how to write Terraform scripts, bash scripting,

8804
12:39:31,408 --> 12:39:36,288
and you need to know at least Python at the basic level.

8805
12:39:38,286 --> 12:39:42,532
Why do you need to have these fundamental development or coding skills?

8806
12:39:43,888 --> 12:39:47,524
Because you need it, you are going to sit in meetings with developers.

8807
12:39:47,790 --> 12:39:50,596
So you need to understand their lingo, what they talk about.

8808
12:39:51,022 --> 12:40:00,848
Also, you are going to write automation scripts, whether it is bash scripts, infrastructure as code, configuration management, or automation through Python,

8809
12:40:01,008 --> 12:40:05,892
all of these are going to be scripting that you are going to scripting skills that you will need.

8810
12:40:07,502 --> 12:40:10,660
And also, you may write scripts using SDKs.

8811
12:40:10,864 --> 12:40:17,764
Python is one of them. So you can deal with AWS and you can deal with different infrastructures through programs that you write in Python.

8812
12:40:17,984 --> 12:40:22,084
Very simple. This is not like when you need to learn everything about Python and become an expert.

8813
12:40:22,478 --> 12:40:26,820
I'm talking about a six to ten hours course that will give you all the required skills.

8814
12:40:27,024 --> 12:40:37,220
So don't freak out and don't leave potential and shiny careers just because you don't want or you are scared to learn.

8815
12:40:38,702 --> 12:40:42,180
Also, for monitoring and alerting scripts, you will need that as well.

8816
12:40:43,118 --> 12:40:51,108
And the recommended coding skills that you need to have for DevOps, Python or Go. Python has, I think, won the deal, so now it's Python and Python.

8817
12:40:51,822 --> 12:40:58,948
Bash or PowerShell, I would go with Bash because most of the servers you are going to deal with in the modern IT are going to be Linux.

8818
12:40:59,438 --> 12:41:03,780
And Terraform for infrastructure as code and Ansible for configuration management.

8819
12:41:04,064 --> 12:41:05,604
These will be the ones that you need to learn.

8820
12:41:08,686 --> 12:41:12,004
As DevOps evolved, we found out there is

8821
12:41:12,814 --> 12:41:15,396
some area that has been overlooked by DevOps.

8822
12:41:15,824 --> 12:41:19,716
And that is once the software is released and deployed,

8823
12:41:20,494 --> 12:41:31,044
who looks after the reliability, the performance, the availability, the uptime, the capacity planning of the infrastructure for the deployed releases and the ones that are coming up?

8824
12:41:31,918 --> 12:41:35,332
Who's going to deal with incident management? This is not DevOps.

8825
12:41:35,790 --> 12:41:36,644
Who's going to be

8826
12:41:37,422 --> 12:41:41,092
awake at 3 o'clock in the morning when there is a problem trying to fix issues?

8827
12:41:41,838 --> 12:41:43,524
That is not DevOps.

8828
12:41:44,206 --> 12:41:52,484
So DevOps overlooked these areas, and that's why the evolution by Google for the site reliability engineering emerged or surfaced,

8829
12:41:52,878 --> 12:41:56,580
and it has been out for quite some time now and it's gaining more popularity.

8830
12:41:57,504 --> 12:42:02,788
Because it looks at the entire thing as a system that needs to be reliable,

8831
12:42:03,566 --> 12:42:06,596
and it focuses on uptime and it focuses on a lot of other things.

8832
12:42:06,784 --> 12:42:11,236
So let's contrast SRE to DevOps and see what are the difference between them.

8833
12:42:13,038 --> 12:42:15,300
So DevOps focuses more on the speed

8834
12:42:16,142 --> 12:42:19,172
than on the stability and reliability of releases.

8835
12:42:19,438 --> 12:42:21,892
So we need to make sure that they don't have bugs, there are no errors,

8836
12:42:22,574 --> 12:42:24,292
and they will be released more frequently.

8837
12:42:24,398 --> 12:42:28,772
But once deployed, maybe they will be performing, maybe they will not be performing as expected.

8838
12:42:29,038 --> 12:42:30,052
So who looks after that?

8839
12:42:30,350 --> 12:42:30,884
Not DevOps.

8840
12:42:31,950 --> 12:42:37,700
DevOps does not define a role that focuses on the overall reliability of the deployed systems or applications.

8841
12:42:39,246 --> 12:42:40,964
SRE will fill this gap.

8842
12:42:41,294 --> 12:42:50,916
So SRE will mainly look at the reliability and the performance and the uptime of releases and applications after DevOps.

8843
12:42:54,830 --> 12:42:57,028
So what would a site reliability engineer

8844
12:42:58,190 --> 12:42:59,044
focus on?

8845
12:42:59,822 --> 12:43:00,804
Incident response

8846
12:43:01,744 --> 12:43:03,744
and reliability metrics.

8847
12:43:04,064 --> 12:43:12,384
So they will have a lot of tools, logging tools, monitoring tools that will look at service level indicators, SLIs, service level

8848
12:43:12,704 --> 12:43:15,428
objective as well as error budgets.

8849
12:43:16,686 --> 12:43:17,572
So that's their focus.

8850
12:43:18,638 --> 12:43:27,428
Root cause analysis. When a problem happens, finding out what happened and why it happened is also what the SRE should do.

8851
12:43:28,078 --> 12:43:34,820
So this is the one that will be notified at 3 o'clock in the morning to wake up and see what the problem is and fix problems and all that.

8852
12:43:35,726 --> 12:43:37,924
And this is usually a team, not an individual.

8853
12:43:40,526 --> 12:43:42,916
If we contrast DevOps and SRE,

8854
12:43:44,046 --> 12:43:48,000
the focus of SRE is reliability and uptime and performance.

8855
12:43:48,430 --> 12:43:54,800
Whereas the DevOps is the faster delivery of software and the frequent releases.

8856
12:43:55,534 --> 12:44:02,820
SRE will be more into the operation side, incident response, monitoring and alerting and capacity planning for the infrastructure.

8857
12:44:03,470 --> 12:44:12,560
Whereas DevOps will focus on CICD, release automation, automating the process, environment builds to deploy the software on,

8858
12:44:12,720 --> 12:44:16,132
and code configuration management as well as infrastructure as code.

8859
12:44:16,910 --> 12:44:19,012
So SRE and DevOps are different.

8860
12:44:19,726 --> 12:44:22,564
Skills are different. However, the core skills are the same.

8861
12:44:23,118 --> 12:44:30,244
You can't expect the SRE to go ahead and fix a problem in a Kubernetes cluster that is faulty without knowing Kubernetes.

8862
12:44:31,214 --> 12:44:36,676
And you don't expect them as well that they are going to work or troubleshoot something in the cloud if they don't know the cloud.

8863
12:44:37,422 --> 12:44:41,348
They would go into systems and find out why it happened and look at logs.

8864
12:44:41,440 --> 12:44:46,500
So they have to know about logging solutions, they have to know about Linux, they have to know about Bash.

8865
12:44:47,150 --> 12:44:50,596
So they have to know about a lot of things or the common skills are the same.

8866
12:44:50,926 --> 12:44:58,788
However, when it comes to monitoring, uptime, capacity planning, and so on, these are specialized tools that are used by SREs more than DevOps.

8867
12:45:00,718 --> 12:45:08,708
DevOps is going to focus on tooling or tools and pipelines that help developers in the software development lifecycle to build,

8868
12:45:09,134 --> 12:45:13,028
test, and deploy code quickly and automatically with less errors and high quality.

8869
12:45:13,742 --> 12:45:24,164
Whereas SREs will focus on keeping the systems reliable, the applications deployed, the software deployed, and make sure that it is scalable and available in terms of uptime.

8870
12:45:24,782 --> 12:45:32,484
So they are going to use software engineering and tools in order to monitor these criteria or metrics and make sure they are up to expectations.

8871
12:45:35,182 --> 12:45:40,420
DevOps will include CICD, infrastructure as code, configuration management, automated testing and monitoring.

8872
12:45:41,102 --> 12:45:44,580
To an extent, not the full-fledged monitoring like SRE.

8873
12:45:45,294 --> 12:45:53,412
Whereas SRE will include things like the error budgets, service level indicators, service level objectives, that will be the focus of SREs.

8874
